    {'id':'y2-t4-w2-l1','year':'Y2','term':'T4',     'week':2,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 7','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'Adding –s after y',     'explanation':"These rules apply to nouns and verbs. When the word ends in a vowel + y, we simply add the letter 's' to the end of the word. For example, the word donkey becomes donkeys. When a word ends in a consonant + y, we must first change the letter 'y' to an 'i' before we add -es. For example, the word carry becomes carries. When applying these rules to nouns, we are forming a plural noun (e.g. keys) from a singular noun (e.g. key).",     'hlWords':['boy', 'key', 'play', 'boys', 'keys', 'plays', 'annoy', 'valley', 'buy', 'buys', 'annoys', 'valleys', 'stay', 'stays', 'monkey', 'monkeys', 'enjoy', 'enjoys', 'delay', 'delays', 'way', 'ways', 'toys', 'donkey', 'donkeys']},
