    {'id':'y2-t4-w2-l4','year':'Y2','term':'T4',     'week':2,'weekLesson':4,     'sequence':'Mastery 5','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'/igh/ spelt y & /ee/ spelt ey',     'explanation':'The /igh/ phoneme is spelt igh when the /igh/ sound is in the middle of a word and followed by a consonant (often a t). For example, in the words light and sight. The /igh/ phoneme is often spelt y at the end of a short, one syllable word. For example, in the words my, fly and sky. However, it can also be used at the end of longer, multi-syllabic words such as; apply and justify. The /ee/ phoneme is usually spelt ey at the end of a word. These words are often two-syllable words, for example; jockey, chimney and valley.',     'hlWords':['fly', 'cry', 'dry', 'simplify', 'key', 'alley', 'chimney', 'hockey', 'medley', 'spy', 'multiply', 'money', 'fry', 'journey', 'identify', 'abbey', 'donkey', 'jersey', 'shy', 'rely', "donkey's", 'paisley', 'pry', 'my', 'sky', 'apply', 'justify', 'jockey', 'valley']},
    {'id':'y2-t4-w2-l5','year':'Y2','term':'T4',     'week':2,'weekLesson':5,     'sequence':'Mastery 5','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'/igh/ spelt y & /ee/ spelt ey',     'explanation':'The /igh/ phoneme is spelt igh when the /igh/ sound is in the middle of a word and followed by a consonant (often a t). For example, in the words light and sight. The /igh/ phoneme is often spelt y at the end of a short, one syllable word. For example, in the words my, fly and sky. However, it can also be used at the end of longer, multi-syllabic words such as; apply and justify. The /ee/ phoneme is usually spelt ey at the end of a word. These words are often two-syllable words, for example; jockey, chimney and valley.',     'hlWords':['sly', 'classify', 'why', 'sky', 'pulley', 'trolley', 'fly', 'valley', 'justify', 'shy', 'jockey', 'amplify', 'volley', 'donkey', 'try', 'multiply', 'my', 'apply', 'chimney']},
    {'id':'y2-t4-w3-l1','year':'Y2','term':'T4',     'week':3,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 8','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'adding vowel suffixes after consonant + y',     'explanation':'When a word ends in a vowel + y, we simply add the vowel suffix. For example, play becomes playing or played or player. When a word ends in a consonant + y, we simply add the -ing suffix. For example, carry becomes carrying and reply becomes replying. This rule changes when adding the suffixes -ed, -er and -est. For these suffixes, when a word ends in a consonant + y, we change the y to i before we add the suffix. For example, the word carry becomes carried and the word happy becomes happier. When a word ends in a consonant + e, we remove the e before we add a vowel suffix (-ed/-est/-er/-ing/-y). For example, the word move becomes moving and the word shine becomes shiny.',     'hlWords':['copy', 'cry', 'hurry', 'copying', 'crying', 'hurrying', 'copied', 'cried', 'hurried', 'supply', 'dry', 'drying', 'dried', 'supplying', 'supplied', 'study', 'spy', 'carry', 'spying', 'spied', 'studying', 'studied', 'carrying', 'carried', 'marrying', 'fried', 'reply', 'replying', 'happy', 'happier']},
