    {'id':'y2-t4-w3-l4','year':'Y2','term':'T4',     'week':3,'weekLesson':4,     'sequence':'Mastery 6','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'Words ending in -le/-el/-al/-il',     'explanation':'The -le ending is the most commonly used of the endings in this unit. It usually follows a consonant (other than those listed for the -el ending). The -le ending is usually used when the last syllable of the word is unstressed. The -el ending is often used after the letters m, n, r, s, v or w. Examples of these include camel, tunnel, squirrel, tinsel, travel and towel. The -al ending is often applied when the word is an adjective. An example of this would include magical (where the noun magic has formed the adjective magical). There is no clear rule for the application of the -il ending (e.g. pencil). These words must simply be learned.',     'hlWords':['bubble', 'muscle', 'candle', 'simple', 'handle', 'flannel', 'towel', 'camel', 'squirrel', 'unravel', 'special', 'local', 'musical', 'logical', 'natural', 'evil', 'civil', 'April', 'stencil', 'utensil', 'pencil', 'magical', 'giggle', 'pixel', 'apple', 'seasonal', 'nostril', 'possible', 'tropical', 'tunnel', 'tinsel', 'travel']},
