    {'id':'y2-t4-w5-l1','year':'Y2','term':'T4',     'week':5,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 10','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'Doubling consonants when adding suffixes',     'explanation':"This rule is complex because it contains several considerations and skills: 1. The number of syllables in the word 2. The final two letters of the word 3. Knowledge of vowels and consonants 4. Knowledge of vowel suffixes For the purposes of vowel suffix rules, the letter 'y' is considered a vowel when applied as a suffix. When a word with one syllable ends in a single short vowel + consonant, we double the consonant before adding the vowel suffix (-ed/-ing/-er/-est/-y). For example, the word jump ends in consonant + consonant. Therefore, we do not double the consonant (e.g. jumped or jumping). The word drop ends in single short vowel (o) + consonant (p). Therefore, we DO double the consonant (e.g. dropped or dropping).",     'hlWords':['tap', 'hum', 'stop', 'tapped', 'hummed', 'stopped', 'tapping', 'humming', 'stopping', 'flat', 'flatter', 'snap', 'snapped', 'shop', 'shopping', 'thin', 'thinnest', 'pat', 'patting', 'flip', 'flipped', 'sit', 'sitting', 'win', 'winner', 'run', 'runny', 'bigger', 'beginning', 'jump', 'jumped', 'jumping', 'drop', 'dropped', 'dropping']},
