    {'id':'y2-t4-w5-l4','year':'Y2','term':'T4',     'week':5,'weekLesson':4,     'sequence':'Mastery 8','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'adding vowel suffixes after consonant + y',     'explanation':'When a word ends in a vowel + y, we simply add the vowel suffix. For example, play becomes playing or played or player. When a word ends in a consonant + y, we simply add the -ing suffix. For example, carry becomes carrying and reply becomes replying. This rule changes when adding the suffixes -ed, -er and -est. For these suffixes, when a word ends in a consonant + y, we change the y to i before we add the suffix. For example, the word carry becomes carried and the word happy becomes happier. When a word ends in a consonant + e, we remove the e before we add a vowel suffix (-ed/-est/-er/-ing/-y). For example, the word move becomes moving and the word shine becomes shiny.',     'hlWords':['copy', 'reply', 'multiply', 'copying', 'replying', 'multiplying', 'copied', 'replied', 'multiplied', 'dry', 'angry', 'lumpy', 'drier', 'angrier', 'lumpier', 'driest', 'angriest', 'lumpiest', 'cry', 'windy', 'party', 'try', 'trying', 'partied', 'crier', 'windiest', 'lazy', 'hairy', 'fly', 'scurry', 'flying', 'laziest', 'scurried', 'hairier', 'hurried', 'carry', 'carrying', 'carried', 'happy', 'happier']},
    {'id':'y2-t4-w5-l5','year':'Y2','term':'T4',     'week':5,'weekLesson':5,     'sequence':'Mastery 8','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'adding vowel suffixes after consonant + y',     'explanation':'When a word ends in a vowel + y, we simply add the vowel suffix. For example, play becomes playing or played or player. When a word ends in a consonant + y, we simply add the -ing suffix. For example, carry becomes carrying and reply becomes replying. This rule changes when adding the suffixes -ed, -er and -est. For these suffixes, when a word ends in a consonant + y, we change the y to i before we add the suffix. For example, the word carry becomes carried and the word happy becomes happier. When a word ends in a consonant + e, we remove the e before we add a vowel suffix (-ed/-est/-er/-ing/-y). For example, the word move becomes moving and the word shine becomes shiny.',     'hlWords':['carry', 'tidy', 'qualify', 'carrying', 'tidying', 'qualifying', 'copy', 'reply', 'multiply', 'copied', 'replied', 'multiplied', 'drier', 'angrier', 'lumpier', 'driest', 'angriest', 'lumpiest', 'bendy', 'stormy', 'spy', 'spying', 'bendier', 'stormiest', 'married', 'tidier', 'worried', 'copying', 'busiest', 'hurrying', 'satisfied', 'replying', 'carried', 'happy', 'happier']},
    {'id':'y2-t5-w1-l1','year':'Y2','term':'T5',     'week':1,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 11','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'Adding the suffix -ful',     'explanation':'The suffix \'ful\' means "full of". For example, helpful means full of help, careful means full of care and joyful means full of joy. We add –ful to the end of a root word. The spelling of the root word usually stays the same. -ful is always spelt with only one l.',     'hlWords':['painful', 'helpful', 'cheerful', 'thankful', 'colourful', 'peaceful', 'meaningful', 'painless', 'helpless', 'worthless']},
    {'id':'y2-t5-w1-l2','year':'Y2','term':'T5',     'week':1,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 11','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'Adding the suffix -less',     'explanation':'The suffix \'ful\' means "full of". For example, helpful means full of help, careful means full of care and joyful means full of joy. We add –ful to the end of a root word. The spelling of the root word usually stays the same. -ful is always spelt with only one l. The suffix \'less\' means \'without\'. For example, careless means without care, fearless means without fear and harmless means without harm. We add – less to the end of a root word. The spelling of the root word usually stays the same. -less is always spelt with two s letters.',     'hlWords':['painless', 'helpless', 'worthless', 'thankless', 'colourless', 'selfless', 'harmless', 'painful', 'helpful', 'cheerful']},
    {'id':'y2-t5-w1-l3','year':'Y2','term':'T5',     'week':1,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 11','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'Adding the suffix –ful and -less',     'explanation':'The suffix \'ful\' means "full of". For example, helpful means full of help, careful means full of care and joyful means full of joy. We add –ful to the end of a root word. The spelling of the root word usually stays the same. -ful is always spelt with only one l. The suffix \'less\' means \'without\'. For example, careless means without care, fearless means without fear and harmless means without harm. We add – less to the end of a root word. The spelling of the root word usually stays the same. -less is always spelt with two s letters. Remind children that adding the suffix \'ful\' means "full of" and \'less\' means "without".',     'hlWords':['painful', 'painless', 'careful', 'careless', 'helpful', 'helpless', 'useful', 'useless', 'restful', 'restless', 'playful', 'fearless']},
    {'id':'y2-t5-w1-l4','year':'Y2','term':'T5',     'week':1,'weekLesson':4,     'sequence':'Mastery 9','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'adding vowel suffixes after consonant + e',     'explanation':'When a word ends in a consonant + e, we remove the e before we add a vowel suffix (-ed/-est/-er/-ing/-y). For example, the word move becomes moving and the word shine becomes shiny.',     'hlWords':['gym', 'digit', 'gentle', 'stage', 'wage', 'budge', 'ledge', 'used', 'raking', 'lose', 'becoming', 'writer', 'hoped', 'hiding', 'inviting', 'making', 'baking', 'timing']},
    {'id':'y2-t5-w1-l5','year':'Y2','term':'T5',     'week':1,'weekLesson':5,     'sequence':'Mastery 9','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'adding vowel suffixes after consonant + e',     'explanation':'When a word ends in a consonant + e, we remove the e before we add a vowel suffix (-ed/-est/-er/-ing/-y). For example, the word move becomes moving and the word shine becomes shiny.',     'hlWords':['energy', 'digital', 'gent', 'cage', 'huge', 'fridge', 'ledge', 'shiny', 'icy', 'noisy', 'slimy', 'safer', 'smoky', 'giving', 'bravest', 'noticed', 'flamed', 'spicy', 'coming', 'tasty', 'hoping']},
    {'id':'y2-t5-w2-l1','year':'Y2','term':'T5',     'week':2,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 12','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'Adding the suffix -ment',     'explanation':"The suffix 'ment' turns a verb into a noun. It usually means: -The act of doing something -The result of an action -The state of something. For example, the verb improve changes to the noun improvement and the verb enjoy changes to the noun enjoyment. No change is needed to the spelling of the root word.",     'hlWords':['achievement', 'amusement', 'agreement', 'payment', 'enjoyment', 'treatment', 'punishment', 'weakness', 'laziness', 'illness']},
    {'id':'y2-t5-w2-l2','year':'Y2','term':'T5',     'week':2,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 12','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'Adding the suffix –ment & -ness',     'explanation':"The suffix 'ment' turns a verb into a noun. It usually means: -The act of doing something -The result of an action -The state of something. For example, the verb 'improve' changes to the noun 'improvement' and the verb 'enjoy' changes to the noun 'enjoyment'. No change is needed to the spelling of the root word. The suffix 'ness' turns an adjective into a noun. It shows the stage, quality or condition of something. For example, the adjective 'kind' turns into 'kindness' meaning the quality of being kind. If the adjective ends in a consonant + y, change the y to I before adding –ness. If the adjective ends in a vowel + y, keep the y.",     'hlWords':['weakness', 'laziness', 'illness', 'business', 'craziness', 'sadness', 'angriness', 'enjoyment', 'achievement', 'amusement']},
    {'id':'y2-t5-w2-l3','year':'Y2','term':'T5',     'week':2,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 12','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'Adding the suffix –ment, -ness & -ly',     'explanation':"The suffix 'ment' turns a verb into a noun. It usually means: -The act of doing something -The result of an action -The state of something. For example, the verb 'improve' changes to the noun 'improvement' and the verb 'enjoy' changes to the noun 'enjoyment'. No change is needed to the spelling of the root word. The suffix 'ness' turns an adjective into a noun. It shows the stage, quality or condition of something. For example, the adjective 'kind' turns into 'kindness' meaning the quality of being kind. If the adjective ends in a consonant + y, change the y to I before adding –ness. If the adjective ends in a vowel + y, keep the y. The suffix 'ly' usually turns an adjective into an adverb. It tells us how something happens. For most adjectives, we simply add –ly to the root word. For example, quick becomes quickly. When adjectives end in a consonant + y, we change the y to an I before adding ly. For example, happy becomes happily.",     'hlWords':['quietly', 'angrily', 'poorly', 'busily', 'slowly', 'calmness', 'happiness', 'arrangement', 'enjoyment', 'kindly']},
    {'id':'y2-t5-w2-l4','year':'Y2','term':'T5',     'week':2,'weekLesson':4,     'sequence':'Mastery 10','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'Doubling consonants when adding suffixes',     'explanation':"This rule is complex because it contains several considerations and skills: 1. The number of syllables in the word 2. The final two letters of the word 3. Knowledge of vowels and consonants 4. Knowledge of vowel suffixes For the purposes of vowel suffix rules, the letter 'y' is considered a vowel when applied as a suffix. When a word with one syllable ends in a single short vowel + consonant, we double the consonant before adding the vowel suffix (-ed/-ing/-er/-est/-y). For example, the word jump ends in consonant + consonant. Therefore, we do not double the consonant (e.g. jumped or jumping). The word drop ends in single short vowel (o) + consonant (p). Therefore, we DO double the consonant (e.g. dropped or dropping).",     'hlWords':['pence', 'prance', 'accent', 'stencil', 'civil', 'citrus', 'cycle', 'mercy', 'icy', 'dropped', 'shopping', 'flatter', 'sunny', 'runner', 'mapped', 'muddy', 'winning', 'biggest', 'sitting', 'hotter']},
    {'id':'y2-t5-w2-l5','year':'Y2','term':'T5',     'week':2,'weekLesson':5,     'sequence':'Mastery 10','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'Doubling consonants when adding suffixes',     'explanation':"This rule is complex because it contains several considerations and skills: 1. The number of syllables in the word 2. The final two letters of the word 3. Knowledge of vowels and consonants 4. Knowledge of vowel suffixes For the purposes of vowel suffix rules, the letter 'y' is considered a vowel when applied as a suffix. When a word with one syllable ends in a single short vowel + consonant, we double the consonant before adding the vowel suffix (-ed/-ing/-er/-est/-y). For example, the word jump ends in consonant + consonant. Therefore, we do not double the consonant (e.g. jumped or jumping). The word drop ends in single short vowel (o) + consonant (p). Therefore, we DO double the consonant (e.g. dropped or dropping).",     'hlWords':['cell', 'certain', 'certificate', 'city', 'cinema', 'circulate', 'cycle', 'infancy', 'cylinder', 'sweetest', 'wetter', 'picky', 'treated', 'frothy', 'winner', 'hunting', 'spotted', 'stronger', 'wagged', 'yelped', 'owner']},
    {'id':'y2-t5-w3-l1','year':'Y2','term':'T5',     'week':3,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 13','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'contractions',     'explanation':"Apostrophes can be used for two reasons: contractions and possession. When we combine two words to form a contraction, the apostrophe is used to replace the missing letter(s). For example, when we combine do + not, we omit the second letter 'o'. Therefore, this is where the apostrophe is placed – don't. When we use apostrophes for possession, we need to consider who the 'owner' is. This is an example sentence: I borrowed the teacher's book. In this sentence, the book is 'owned' by the teacher. Therefore, we place the apostrophe in the word teacher's. The apostrophe is placed before the letter 's' to show that one person has possession – this is known as singular possession. Note: A common misconception for children is that the word 'its' always contains an apostrophe. It should only contain an apostrophe if the word can be changed to it is.",     'hlWords':["didn't", "they're", "she's", "he's", "aren't", "we're", "weren't", "you're", "that's", "there's", "it's", "shouldn't", "hadn't"]},
