    {'id':'y2-t6-w1-l1','year':'Y2','term':'T6',     'week':1,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 16','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':'Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings.\n\nyour / you’re\nThe word your is possessive. It indicates that something belongs to you. Please hand in your book. The word you’re is a contraction of you are. I know that you’re trying hard.\n\nthere / their / they’re\nThe word there refers to a place or can be used to point out something. Put your shoes over there. The word their shows possession. It indicates that someone owns something. We visited their house yesterday. The word they’re is a contraction of they are. They told me they’re going to be late.\n\nto / two / too\nThe word to is a preposition. It shows a direction or place. It can also form part of a verb phrase. I am going to the shop. The word two is a number. My neighbour owns two cats. The word too means ’as well’ or ’more than enough’. I am going there too. I have eaten too much cake.',     'hlWords':['you’re', 'your', 'there', 'their', 'they’re', 'to', 'too', 'brother', 'two', 'mother']},
