    {'id':'y2-t6-w1-l3','year':'Y2','term':'T6',     'week':1,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 16','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':'Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings.\n\nyour / you’re\nThe word your is possessive. It indicates that something belongs to you. Please hand in your book. The word you’re is a contraction of you are. I know that you’re trying hard.\n\nthere / their / they’re\nThe word there refers to a place or can be used to point out something. Put your shoes over there. The word their shows possession. It indicates that someone owns something. We visited their house yesterday. The word they’re is a contraction of they are. They told me they’re going to be late.\n\nto / two / too\nThe word to is a preposition. It shows a direction or place. It can also form part of a verb phrase. I am going to the shop. The word two is a number. My neighbour owns two cats. The word too means ’as well’ or ’more than enough’. I am going there too. I have eaten too much cake.',     'hlWords':['you’re', 'your', 'there', 'their', 'they’re', 'to', 'too', 'two', 'brother', 'mother']},
    {'id':'y2-t6-w1-l4','year':'Y2','term':'T6',     'week':1,'weekLesson':4,     'sequence':'Mastery 14','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'Possessive singular apostrophe',     'explanation':'Apostrophes can be used for two reasons: contractions and possession. When we combine two words to form a contraction, the apostrophe is used to replace the missing letter(s). For example, when we combine do + not, we omit the second letter ’o’. Therefore, this is where the apostrophe is placed – don’t. When we use apostrophes for possession, we need to consider who the ’owner’ is. This is an example sentence: I borrowed the teacher’s book. In this sentence, the book is ’owned’ by the teacher. Therefore, we place the apostrophe in the word teacher’s. The apostrophe is placed before the letter ’s’ to show that one person has possession – this is known as singular possession.\n\nNote:\nA common misconception for children is that the word ’its’ always contains an apostrophe. It should only contain an apostrophe if the word can be changed to it is.',     'hlWords':['emotional', 'edible', 'April', 'channel', 'pencil', 'novel', 'suitcase’s', 'sunflower’s', 'dog’s', 'baby’s', 'cat’s', 'owner’s']},
    {'id':'y2-t6-w1-l5','year':'Y2','term':'T6',     'week':1,'weekLesson':5,     'sequence':'Mastery 14','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'Possessive singular apostrophe',     'explanation':'Apostrophes can be used for two reasons: contractions and possession. When we combine two words to form a contraction, the apostrophe is used to replace the missing letter(s). For example, when we combine do + not, we omit the second letter ’o’. Therefore, this is where the apostrophe is placed – don’t. When we use apostrophes for possession, we need to consider who the ’owner’ is. This is an example sentence: I borrowed the teacher’s book. In this sentence, the book is ’owned’ by the teacher. Therefore, we place the apostrophe in the word teacher’s. The apostrophe is placed before the letter ’s’ to show that one person has possession – this is known as singular possession.\n\nNote:\nA common misconception for children is that the word ’its’ always contains an apostrophe. It should only contain an apostrophe if the word can be changed to it is.',     'hlWords':['towel', 'muscle', 'special', 'local', 'evil', 'simple', 'Sarah’s', 'school’s', 'parent’s', 'son’s', 'teacher’s', 'pupil’s']},
    {'id':'y2-t6-w2-l1','year':'Y2','term':'T6',     'week':2,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 17','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'/u/ spelt o',     'explanation':'The /u/ phoneme is often spelt with an o before n, m, th and v.\n\nFor example, in the words: son, money, some, among, mother, other, love and dove',     'hlWords':['mother', 'month', 'won', 'brother', 'front', 'wonder', 'nothing', 'another', 'love', 'son']},
