    {'id':'y2-t6-w2-l4','year':'Y2','term':'T6',     'week':2,'weekLesson':4,     'sequence':'Mastery 15','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'Words ending -tion',     'explanation':'When we add –tion to a verb it changes it into a noun. It usually means “the act” or “the result”. If the word already ends in ’t’ just add ’ion’ (together it looks like ’tion’). For example, act → action and invent → invention. If the word ends in ’te’ drop the ’e’ and add ’ion’. For example, promote → promotion.\n\nOther less-common rules:\nIf the word ends in ’ite’, change it to ’ation’. For example, invite → invitation. If the word ends in ’ify’, change it to ’ification’. For example, simplify → simplification and classify → classification. If the word ends in ’ise’, drop the ’e’ and add ’isation’. For example, realise → realisation and organise → organisation.',     'hlWords':['toys', 'parties', 'berries', 'holidays', 'stories', 'guys', 'rejection', 'reflection', 'selection', 'connection', 'question', 'action', 'invention']},
    {'id':'y2-t6-w2-l5','year':'Y2','term':'T6',     'week':2,'weekLesson':5,     'sequence':'Mastery 15','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'Words ending -tion',     'explanation':'When we add –tion to a verb it changes it into a noun. It usually means “the act” or “the result”. If the word already ends in ’t’ just add ’ion’ (together it looks like ’tion’). For example, act → action and invent → invention. If the word ends in ’te’ drop the ’e’ and add ’ion’. For example, promote → promotion.\n\nOther less-common rules:\nIf the word ends in ’ite’, change it to ’ation’. For example, invite → invitation. If the word ends in ’ify’, change it to ’ification’. For example, simplify → simplification and classify → classification. If the word ends in ’ise’, drop the ’e’ and add ’isation’. For example, realise → realisation and organise → organisation.',     'hlWords':['days', 'enemies', 'countries', 'hobbies', 'plays', 'families', 'pollination', 'hesitation', 'location', 'decoration', 'celebration', 'collection', 'population']},
    {'id':'y2-t6-w3-l1','year':'Y2','term':'T6',     'week':3,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 18','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'/or/ spelt a before l and ll',     'explanation':'The /or/ phoneme is often spelt a before l or ll in common words. For example, in the words all, ball, call, fall, wall, small and tall.\n\nIn many accents, this spelling can also be seen in words like walk and talk, where the sound changes over time.\n\nHowever, this spelling does not always make the /or/ sound. In other words with al, such as animal, metal, final and local, the pronunciation is different and does not sound like /or/.',     'hlWords':['taller', 'beanstalk', 'talk', 'chalk', 'hospital', 'ball', 'hallway', 'smallest', 'walk', 'hall', 'wall']},
    {'id':'y2-t6-w3-l2','year':'Y2','term':'T6',     'week':3,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 18','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'/or/ spelt ar',     'explanation':'The /or/ phoneme is often spelt a before l or ll in common words. For example, in the words all, ball, call, fall, wall, small and tall.\n\nIn many accents, this spelling can also be seen in words like walk and talk, where the sound changes over time.\n\nHowever, this spelling does not always make the /or/ sound. In other words with al, such as animal, metal, final and local, the pronunciation is different and does not sound like /or/.\n\nThe /or/ phoneme has also be spelt ar following w or qu. For example, in the words warm and quarry. Words which use this spelling are usually war, time or number words. However, there are not many words which use this spelling.',     'hlWords':['warmth', 'ward', 'warmest', 'award', 'rewarded', 'Quartz', 'taller', 'beanstalk', 'talk', 'chalk']},
    {'id':'y2-t6-w3-l3','year':'Y2','term':'T6',     'week':3,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 18','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'/or/ spelt a and ar',     'explanation':'The /or/ phoneme is often spelt a before l or ll in common words. For example, in the words all, ball, call, fall, wall, small and tall.\n\nIn many accents, this spelling can also be seen in words like walk and talk, where the sound changes over time.\n\nHowever, this spelling does not always make the /or/ sound. In other words with al, such as animal, metal, final and local, the pronunciation is different and does not sound like /or/.\n\nThe /or/ phoneme has also be spelt ar following w or qu. For example, in the words warm and quarry. Words which use this spelling are usually war, time or number words. However, there are not many words which use this spelling.',     'hlWords':['wallflower', 'wardrobe', 'talking', 'awards', 'fallen', 'quarter', 'swarm', 'taller', 'beanstalk', 'talk']},
