    {'id':'y3-t1-w1-l1','year':'Y3','term':'T1',     'week':1,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'words ending -le/-el/-al/-il',     'explanation':'The -le ending is the most commonly used of the endings in this unit. It usually follows a consonant (other than those listed for the -el ending). The -le ending is usually used when the last syllable of the word is unstressed. The -el ending is often used after the letters m, n, r, s, v or w. Examples of these include camel, tunnel, squirrel, tinsel, travel and towel. The -al ending is often applied when the word is an adjective. An example of this would include magical (where the noun magic has formed the adjective magical). There is no clear rule for the application of the -il ending (e.g. pencil). These words must simply be learned.',     'hlWords':['paddle', 'beetle', 'enamel', 'tunnel', 'squirrel', 'jingle', 'stable', 'swivel', 'uncle', 'camel', 'puzzle', 'puddle']},
    {'id':'y3-t1-w1-l2','year':'Y3','term':'T1',     'week':1,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'words ending -le/-el/-al/-il',     'explanation':'The -le ending is the most commonly used of the endings in this unit. It usually follows a consonant (other than those listed for the -el ending). The -le ending is usually used when the last syllable of the word is unstressed. The -el ending is often used after the letters m, n, r, s, v or w. Examples of these include camel, tunnel, squirrel, tinsel, travel and towel. The -al ending is often applied when the word is an adjective. An example of this would include magical (where the noun magic has formed the adjective magical). There is no clear rule for the application of the -il ending (e.g. pencil). These words must simply be learned.',     'hlWords':['shovel', 'national', 'example', 'humble', 'novel', 'royal', 'vessel', 'magical', 'tickle', 'personal', 'single', 'gravel']},
    {'id':'y3-t1-w1-l3','year':'Y3','term':'T1',     'week':1,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'words ending -le/-el/-al/-il',     'explanation':'The -le ending is the most commonly used of the endings in this unit. It usually follows a consonant (other than those listed for the -el ending). The -le ending is usually used when the last syllable of the word is unstressed. The -el ending is often used after the letters m, n, r, s, v or w. Examples of these include camel, tunnel, squirrel, tinsel, travel and towel. The -al ending is often applied when the word is an adjective. An example of this would include magical (where the noun magic has formed the adjective magical). There is no clear rule for the application of the -il ending (e.g. pencil). These words must simply be learned.',     'hlWords':['channel', 'handle', 'musical', 'evil', 'pupil', 'pencil', 'civil', 'fossil', 'council', 'basil', 'April', 'nostril', 'stencil', 'gravel', 'magical', 'candle', 'tunnel']},
    {'id':'y3-t1-w2-l1','year':'Y3','term':'T1',     'week':2,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 4',     'focus':'adding -es after y',     'explanation':"These rules apply to nouns and verbs. When the word ends in a vowel + y, we simply add the letter 's' to the end of the word. For example, the word donkey becomes donkeys. When a word ends in a consonant + y, we must first change the letter 'y' to an 'i' before we add -es. For example, the word carry becomes carries. When applying these rules to nouns, we are forming a plural noun (e.g. keys) from a singular noun (e.g. key).",     'hlWords':['valleys', 'pennies', 'cities', 'parties', 'destroys', 'enemies', 'chimneys', 'libraries', 'countries', 'bodies', 'annoys', 'families']},
    {'id':'y3-t1-w2-l2','year':'Y3','term':'T1',     'week':2,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 5',     'focus':'adding -es after y',     'explanation':"These rules apply to nouns and verbs. When the word ends in a vowel + y, we simply add the letter 's' to the end of the word. For example, the word donkey becomes donkeys. When a word ends in a consonant + y, we must first change the letter 'y' to an 'i' before we add -es. For example, the word carry becomes carries. When applying these rules to nouns, we are forming a plural noun (e.g. keys) from a singular noun (e.g. key).",     'hlWords':['parties', 'stories', 'obeys', 'cities', 'monkeys', 'jellies', 'mysteries', 'surveys', 'armies', 'fairies', 'berries', 'puppies', 'holidays']},
    {'id':'y3-t1-w2-l3','year':'Y3','term':'T1',     'week':2,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 6',     'focus':'adding -es after y',     'explanation':"These rules apply to nouns and verbs. When the word ends in a vowel + y, we simply add the letter 's' to the end of the word. For example, the word donkey becomes donkeys. When a word ends in a consonant + y, we must first change the letter 'y' to an 'i' before we add -es. For example, the word carry becomes carries. When applying these rules to nouns, we are forming a plural noun (e.g. keys) from a singular noun (e.g. key).",     'hlWords':['delays', 'families', 'diaries', 'enemies', 'hobbies', 'cowboys', 'toys', 'countries', 'runways', 'puppies', 'nurseries', 'enjoys']},
    {'id':'y3-t1-w3-l1','year':'Y3','term':'T1',     'week':3,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 7',     'focus':'adding vowel suffixes',     'explanation':'When a word ends in a vowel + y, we simply add the vowel suffix. For example, play becomes playing or played or player. When a word ends in a consonant + y, we simply add the -ing suffix. For example, carry becomes carrying and reply becomes replying. This rule changes when adding the suffixes -ed, -er and -est. For these suffixes, when a word ends in a consonant + y, we change the y to i before we add the suffix. For example, the word carry becomes carried and the word happy becomes happier. When a word ends in a consonant + e, we remove the e before we add a vowel suffix (-ed/-est/-er/-ing/-y). For example, the word move becomes moving and the word shine becomes shiny.',     'hlWords':['drying', 'supplying', 'identifying', 'dried', 'supplied', 'identified', 'carrying', 'carried', 'burying', 'buried', 'terrifying', 'terrified', 'replied', 'multiplying', 'married']},
    {'id':'y3-t1-w3-l2','year':'Y3','term':'T1',     'week':3,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 8',     'focus':'adding vowel suffixes',     'explanation':'When a word ends in a vowel + y, we simply add the vowel suffix. For example, play becomes playing or played or player. When a word ends in a consonant + y, we simply add the -ing suffix. For example, carry becomes carrying and reply becomes replying. This rule changes when adding the suffixes -ed, -er and -est. For these suffixes, when a word ends in a consonant + y, we change the y to i before we add the suffix. For example, the word carry becomes carried and the word happy becomes happier. When a word ends in a consonant + e, we remove the e before we add a vowel suffix (-ed/-est/-er/-ing/-y). For example, the word move becomes moving and the word shine becomes shiny.',     'hlWords':['drier', 'prettier', 'scarier', 'driest', 'prettiest', 'scariest', 'luckier', 'luckiest', 'grumpier', 'grumpiest', 'noisier', 'noisiest', 'funnier', 'trickiest', 'sneakier']},
    {'id':'y3-t1-w3-l3','year':'Y3','term':'T1',     'week':3,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 9',     'focus':'adding vowel suffixes',     'explanation':'When a word ends in a vowel + y, we simply add the vowel suffix. For example, play becomes playing or played or player. When a word ends in a consonant + y, we simply add the -ing suffix. For example, carry becomes carrying and reply becomes replying. This rule changes when adding the suffixes -ed, -er and -est. For these suffixes, when a word ends in a consonant + y, we change the y to i before we add the suffix. For example, the word carry becomes carried and the word happy becomes happier. When a word ends in a consonant + e, we remove the e before we add a vowel suffix (-ed/-est/-er/-ing/-y). For example, the word move becomes moving and the word shine becomes shiny.',     'hlWords':['racing', 'hoped', 'shiny', 'smiling', 'closer', 'escaped', 'latest', 'breezy', 'coming', 'bravest', 'amazing', 'confused']},
    {'id':'y3-t1-w4-l1','year':'Y3','term':'T1',     'week':4,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 10',     'focus':'doubling consonants in one-syllable words',     'explanation':"This rule is complex because it contains several considerations and skills: 1. The number of syllables in the word 2. The final two letters of the word 3. Knowledge of vowels and consonants 4. Knowledge of vowel suffixes For the purposes of vowel suffix rules, the letter 'y' is considered a vowel when applied as a suffix. When a word with one syllable ends in a single short vowel + consonant, we double the consonant before adding the vowel suffix (-ed/-ing/-er/-est/-y). For example, the word jump ends in consonant + consonant. Therefore, we do not double the consonant (e.g. jumped or jumping). The word drop ends in single short vowel (o) + consonant (p). Therefore, we DO double the consonant (e.g. dropped or dropping).",     'hlWords':['flattest', 'snapping', 'stopper', 'flipper', 'hottest', 'tapping', 'thinnest', 'jammy', 'planned', 'sunny', 'biggest', 'dropped']},
    {'id':'y3-t1-w4-l2','year':'Y3','term':'T1',     'week':4,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 11',     'focus':'doubling consonants in one-syllable words',     'explanation':"This rule is complex because it contains several considerations and skills: 1. The number of syllables in the word 2. The final two letters of the word 3. Knowledge of vowels and consonants 4. Knowledge of vowel suffixes For the purposes of vowel suffix rules, the letter 'y' is considered a vowel when applied as a suffix. When a word with one syllable ends in a single short vowel + consonant, we double the consonant before adding the vowel suffix (-ed/-ing/-er/-est/-y). For example, the word jump ends in consonant + consonant. Therefore, we do not double the consonant (e.g. jumped or jumping). The word drop ends in single short vowel (o) + consonant (p). Therefore, we DO double the consonant (e.g. dropped or dropping).",     'hlWords':['filming', 'saddest', 'stopped', 'flipped', 'sinking', 'funny', 'flattest', 'hotter', 'bumped', 'thinner', 'camped', 'planning']},
    {'id':'y3-t1-w4-l3','year':'Y3','term':'T1',     'week':4,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 12',     'focus':'doubling consonants in one-syllable words',     'explanation':"This rule is complex because it contains several considerations and skills: 1. The number of syllables in the word 2. The final two letters of the word 3. Knowledge of vowels and consonants 4. Knowledge of vowel suffixes For the purposes of vowel suffix rules, the letter 'y' is considered a vowel when applied as a suffix. When a word with one syllable ends in a single short vowel + consonant, we double the consonant before adding the vowel suffix (-ed/-ing/-er/-est/-y). For example, the word jump ends in consonant + consonant. Therefore, we do not double the consonant (e.g. jumped or jumping). The word drop ends in single short vowel (o) + consonant (p). Therefore, we DO double the consonant (e.g. dropped or dropping).",     'hlWords':['tracked', 'clapping', 'cleaner', 'slipped', 'fattest', 'packed', 'dreaming', 'scanner', 'drippy', 'beamed', 'locking', 'drummer']},
    {'id':'y3-t1-w5-l1','year':'Y3','term':'T1',     'week':5,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 13',     'focus':'apostrophes',     'explanation':"Apostrophes can be used for two reasons: contractions and possession. When we combine two words to form a contraction, the apostrophe is used to replace the missing letter(s). For example, when we combine do + not, we omit the second letter 'o'. Therefore, this is where the apostrophe is placed – don't. When we use apostrophes for possession, we need to consider who the 'owner' is. This is an example sentence: I borrowed the teacher's book. In this sentence, the book is 'owned' by the teacher. Therefore, we place the apostrophe in the word teacher's. The apostrophe is placed before the letter 's' to show that one person has possession – this is known as singular possession. Note: A common misconception for children is that the word 'its' always contains an apostrophe. It should only contain an apostrophe if the word can be changed to it is.",     'hlWords':["don't", "it's", "didn't", "you've", "we'll", "he's", "they've", "she'd", "we're", "doesn't", "you'll", "they're", "they'll", "wasn't", "you're"]},
