    {'id':'y3-t3-w1-l1','year':'Y3','term':'T3',     'week':1,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 3','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'adding the -ous suffix',     'explanation':"The -ous suffix is added to nouns to form adjectives. It means 'full of' or 'having the quality of'. For most root words, simply add -ous (poisonous). If the root word ends in y, change y to i before adding -ous. For example, the word envy becomes envious. If the root word ends in our, change our to or before adding -ous. For example, the word humour becomes humorous. Often, if the root word ends in e, the e should be removed before adding -ous. For example, the word continue becomes continuous. However, if the root word ends in ge, DO NOT remove the e (e.g. outrageous). This is because the e gives the g its 'soft' sound.",     'hlWords':['ruinous', 'thunderous', 'cavernous', 'joyous', 'dangerous', 'venomous', 'perilous', 'villainous', 'poisonous', 'momentous']},
