    {'id':'y3-t3-w3-l1','year':'Y3','term':'T3',     'week':3,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Mastery 1','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'doubling consonants',     'explanation':"When a word with more than one syllable ends in a single short vowel letter + consonant, and the last syllable is stressed we double the consonant before adding the vowel suffix. (A syllable being stressed means that it is the most emphasised syllable – it is louder or clearer than the others when spoken aloud.) The word 'open' ends in a single vowel (e) + single consonant (n). In the word o-pen, it is the first syllable (making the long o sound) that is stressed. Therefore, when we add a vowel suffix, we do not double the final consonant. For example, we simply add the -ed suffix to get opened. The word 'begin' ends in a single vowel (i) + single consonant (n). In the word be-gin, it is the final syllable (gin) that is stressed. Therefore, when we add a vowel suffix, we DO double the final consonant. For example, when we add the -er suffix, we get beginner.",     'hlWords':['occurring', 'controller', 'inference', 'gardening', 'following', 'forgetting', 'beginning', 'opening', 'referring', 'admitted', 'entered', 'happened', 'regretted', 'committed', 'listened', 'followed', 'controlled']},
    {'id':'y3-t3-w3-l2','year':'Y3','term':'T3',     'week':3,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Mastery 1','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'doubling consonants',     'explanation':"When a word with more than one syllable ends in a single short vowel letter + consonant, and the last syllable is stressed we double the consonant before adding the vowel suffix. (A syllable being stressed means that it is the most emphasised syllable – it is louder or clearer than the others when spoken aloud.) The word 'open' ends in a single vowel (e) + single consonant (n). In the word o-pen, it is the first syllable (making the long o sound) that is stressed. Therefore, when we add a vowel suffix, we do not double the final consonant. For example, we simply add the -ed suffix to get opened. The word 'begin' ends in a single vowel (i) + single consonant (n). In the word be-gin, it is the final syllable (gin) that is stressed. Therefore, when we add a vowel suffix, we DO double the final consonant. For example, when we add the -er suffix, we get beginner.",     'hlWords':['forgotten', 'avoiding', 'admitted', 'inference', 'forgetting', 'beginning', 'regretted', 'occurring', 'controller', 'gardening']},
    {'id':'y3-t3-w3-l3','year':'Y3','term':'T3',     'week':3,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Mastery 1','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'doubling consonants',     'explanation':"When a word with more than one syllable ends in a single short vowel letter + consonant, and the last syllable is stressed we double the consonant before adding the vowel suffix. (A syllable being stressed means that it is the most emphasised syllable – it is louder or clearer than the others when spoken aloud.) The word 'open' ends in a single vowel (e) + single consonant (n). In the word o-pen, it is the first syllable (making the long o sound) that is stressed. Therefore, when we add a vowel suffix, we do not double the final consonant. For example, we simply add the -ed suffix to get opened. The word 'begin' ends in a single vowel (i) + single consonant (n). In the word be-gin, it is the final syllable (gin) that is stressed. Therefore, when we add a vowel suffix, we DO double the final consonant. For example, when we add the -er suffix, we get beginner.",     'hlWords':['beginner', 'beginning', 'regretted', 'regretting', 'visited', 'visiting', 'inferred', 'inference', 'offered', 'controlling', 'forgetting']},
    {'id':'y3-t3-w4-l1','year':'Y3','term':'T3',     'week':4,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Mastery 2','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'the -ly suffix',     'explanation':'For most root words, we simply add the -ly suffix (because it begins with a consonant). However, there are some exceptions to this, where other rules apply. If the root word ends in y, change y to i before adding -ly. For example, the word happy becomes happily. If the root word ends in -le, change -le to -ly. For example, the word gentle becomes gently. If the root word ends in -ic, use the -ally suffix (instead of -ly). For example, the word magic becomes magically.',     'hlWords':['logically', 'completely', 'usually', 'frantically', 'weekly', 'perfectly', 'classically', 'honestly', 'leisurely', 'artistically', 'organically', 'cowardly', 'instantly', 'genetically', 'ironically', 'lively', 'musically', 'exactly', 'likely', 'physically', 'friendly', 'basically', 'magically']},
