    {'id':'y3-t4-w3-l1','year':'Y3','term':'T4',     'week':3,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 6','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'alternative long a sounds',     'explanation':'The long a sound has various representations. In this sequence, children are introduced to the following graphemes – ei / eigh / ey. The ei grapheme is often found in the middle of a word. Examples include rein, beige and veil. The ey grapheme is often found at the end of a word. Examples include grey, obey and survey. The eigh grapheme can be found in different positions within words. The most common roots that use this grapheme include eight, weigh and neigh. These roots form many other words containing the same grapheme (e.g. eighteen and neighbour).',     'hlWords':['sleigh', 'eight', 'reindeer', 'grey', 'they', 'reins', 'eighteen', 'weighed', 'vein', 'reign']},
    {'id':'y3-t4-w3-l2','year':'Y3','term':'T4',     'week':3,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 6','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'alternative long a sounds',     'explanation':'The long a sound has various representations. In this sequence, children are introduced to the following graphemes – ei / eigh / ey. The ei grapheme is often found in the middle of a word. Examples include rein, beige and veil. The ey grapheme is often found at the end of a word. Examples include grey, obey and survey. The eigh grapheme can be found in different positions within words. The most common roots that use this grapheme include eight, weigh and neigh. These roots form many other words containing the same grapheme (e.g. eighteen and neighbour).',     'hlWords':['vein', 'rein', 'reign', 'beige', 'eighteen', 'sleigh', 'neighbour', 'they', 'grey', 'survey', 'prey', 'obey', 'veil', 'reindeer', 'eight', 'neigh', 'weight', 'convey', 'weighed']},
    {'id':'y3-t4-w3-l3','year':'Y3','term':'T4',     'week':3,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 6','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'alternative long a sounds',     'explanation':'The long a sound has various representations. In this sequence, children are introduced to the following graphemes – ei / eigh / ey. The ei grapheme is often found in the middle of a word. Examples include rein, beige and veil. The ey grapheme is often found at the end of a word. Examples include grey, obey and survey. The eigh grapheme can be found in different positions within words. The most common roots that use this grapheme include eight, weigh and neigh. These roots form many other words containing the same grapheme (e.g. eighteen and neighbour).',     'hlWords':['eighteen', 'convey', 'reign', 'prey', 'survey', 'vein', 'sleigh', 'beige', 'overweight', 'eight']},
    {'id':'y3-t4-w4-l1','year':'Y3','term':'T4',     'week':4,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 7','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':"Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. your / you're. The word your is possessive. It indicates that something belongs to you. Please hand in your book. The word you're is a contraction of you are. I know that you're trying hard.",     'hlWords':["you're", 'your', 'there', 'their', "they're", 'to', 'too', 'two', 'here', 'hear']},
    {'id':'y3-t4-w4-l2','year':'Y3','term':'T4',     'week':4,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 7','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':"Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. there / their / they're. The word there refers to a place or can be used to point out something. Put your shoes over there. The word their shows possession. It indicates that someone owns something. We visited their house yesterday. The word they're is a contraction of they are. They told me they're going to be late.",     'hlWords':['there', 'their', "they're", "you're", 'your', 'to', 'too', 'two', 'here', 'hear']},
    {'id':'y3-t4-w4-l3','year':'Y3','term':'T4',     'week':4,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 7','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':"Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. to / two / too. The word to is a preposition. It shows a direction or place. It can also form part of a verb phrase. I am going to the shop. The word two is a number. My neighbour owns two cats. The word too means 'as well' or 'more than enough'. I am going there too. I have eaten too much cake.",     'hlWords':['to', 'too', 'two', "you're", 'your', 'there', 'their', "they're", 'here', 'hear']},
    {'id':'y3-t4-w5-l1','year':'Y3','term':'T4',     'week':5,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 7','lesson':'Lesson 4',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':'Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. here / hear. The word here relates to a place or position. Wait here until they arrive. The word hear relates to sound or listening. Can you hear the music? led / lead. The word led is a past tense verb. It refers to guiding someone. The teacher led the class outside. The word lead is a type of metal (often found in pencils). That pencil contains lead.',     'hlWords':['here', 'hear', 'led', 'lead', "you're", 'your', 'there', 'their', "they're", 'to']},
    {'id':'y3-t4-w5-l2','year':'Y3','term':'T4',     'week':5,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 7','lesson':'Lesson 5',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':"Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. male / mail. The word male relates to a person's gender – it refers to a boy or man. The male lion has a large mane. The word mail relates to letters or packages sent by post. I received a letter in the mail. meet / meat. The word meet is a verb. It means to come together with someone. I will meet my friend at school. The word meat is a noun. It refers to the food product derived from an animal. We had meat and vegetables for dinner.",     'hlWords':['male', 'mail', 'meet', 'meat', "you're", 'your', 'there', 'their', "they're", 'to']},
    {'id':'y3-t4-w5-l3','year':'Y3','term':'T4',     'week':5,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 7','lesson':'Lesson 6',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':'Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. great / grate. The word great refers to something that is good or big. She had a great idea. We stood in front of a great mountain. The word grate refers to shredding a food or something annoying. I will grate the cheese. The noise started to grate on me. seen / scene. The word seen is a past tense verb. It means to look at something with your eyes. I have seen dolphins before. The word scene is a noun. It refers to a place or part of a story or play. The final scene of the film was my favourite.',     'hlWords':['great', 'grate', 'seen', 'scene', "you're", 'your', 'there', 'their', "they're", 'to']},
    {'id':'y3-t5-w1-l1','year':'Y3','term':'T5',     'week':1,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 8','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'tion / sion / ssion / cian',     'explanation':"These suffixes are pronounced like 'shun'. The -tion suffix is often used if the root word ends in t or te. For example, the word complete becomes completion. The -sion suffix is often used if the root word ends in d, de or se. For example, the word decide becomes decision. The -ssion suffix is often used if the root word ends in ss or mit. For example, the word admit becomes admission. The -cian suffix is often used if the root word ends in c or cs. For example, the word electric becomes electrician. It is mostly used to indicate a profession – e.g. magician. It is important to note that the ending of the root word (e.g. mit or cs) is removed before adding the suffix. Note that words ending in 'mit' technically end in 't' so checking for the difference between 'mit' and 't' is important.",     'hlWords':['promote', 'promotion', 'detect', 'detection', 'migrate', 'migration', 'invention', 'education', 'collection', 'adoption', 'donation', 'operation', 'direction', 'creation']},
