    {'id':'y3-t5-w4-l1','year':'Y3','term':'T5',     'week':4,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 9','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'LKS2 Word List',     'explanation':'This lesson focuses on the following words from the LKS2 Statutory Word List: eight, eighth, weight, height, straight. The focus of this lesson is on words containing the eigh and aigh quadgraphs. A quadgraph is a grapheme comprised of four letters. In the word eight, the eigh grapheme produces the long a sound. In the word eighth, the eigh grapheme produces the long a sound. In the word weight, the eigh grapheme produces the long a sound. In the word height, the eigh grapheme produces the long i sound. In the word straight, the aigh grapheme produces the long a sound.',     'hlWords':['height', 'straight', 'eighth', 'eight', 'weight', 'though', 'although', 'enough', 'thought', 'through']},
    {'id':'y3-t5-w4-l2','year':'Y3','term':'T5',     'week':4,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 9','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'LKS2 Word List',     'explanation':'This lesson focuses on the following words from the LKS2 Statutory Word List: though, although, enough, through, thought. The focus of this lesson is on words containing the ough quadgraph. A quadgraph is a grapheme comprised of four letters. In the word though, the ough grapheme produces the long o (oa) sound. In the word although, the ough grapheme produces the long o (oa) sound. In the word enough, the ough grapheme produces the uff sound. In the word through, the ough grapheme produces the oo sound. In the word thought, the ough grapheme produces the aw sound.',     'hlWords':['though', 'although', 'enough', 'thought', 'through', 'height', 'straight', 'eighth', 'eight', 'weight']},
    {'id':'y3-t5-w4-l3','year':'Y3','term':'T5',     'week':4,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 9','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'LKS2 Word List',     'explanation':'This lesson focuses on the following words from the LKS2 Statutory Word List: caught, length, strength, strange. The word caught features an unusual quadgraph. The augh quadgraph is used to represent the aw sound. c-augh-t. The words length and strength feature an unusual consonant cluster (ngth). It may be helpful to separate the ng and th in these words. l-eng-th, str-eng-th. The word strange features the long a sound. However, it does not contain a split digraph as there are two letters between the a and e. In addition, the j sound is produced by the letter g.',     'hlWords':['caught', 'strength', 'length', 'strange', 'height', 'straight', 'eighth', 'eight', 'weight', 'though']},
    {'id':'y3-t5-w5-l1','year':'Y3','term':'T5',     'week':5,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 9','lesson':'Lesson 4',     'focus':'LKS2 Word List',     'explanation':'This lesson focuses on the following words from the LKS2 Statutory Word List: breath / breathe / woman / women. breath vs breathe: The word breath is a noun. It is spelled without an e on the end. It refers to the air that you take in (inhale) and let out (exhale). The word breathe is a verb. It is spelled with an e on the end. It refers to the action of taking in air (inhaling) and letting out air (exhaling). woman vs women: The word woman is singular. It is spelled with an a (like man). It refers to one female adult. The word women is a plural. It is spelled with an e (like men). It refers to more than one female adult.',     'hlWords':['breath', 'breathe', 'woman', 'women', 'height', 'straight', 'eighth', 'eight', 'weight', 'though']},
    {'id':'y3-t5-w5-l2','year':'Y3','term':'T5',     'week':5,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 9','lesson':'Lesson 5',     'focus':'LKS2 Word List',     'explanation':'This lesson focuses on the following words from the LKS2 Statutory Word List: complete / describe / extreme / separate. These words all end with a split digraph. complete – long e. describe – long i. extreme – long e. separate – long a. In addition, the word describe begins with the unusual spelling of des (sounding like dis). The word separate contains the er sound spelled ar. (A helpful mnemonic is, "There is a rat in separate.)',     'hlWords':['describe', 'separate', 'complete', 'extreme', 'height', 'straight', 'eighth', 'eight', 'weight', 'though']},
    {'id':'y3-t5-w5-l3','year':'Y3','term':'T5',     'week':5,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 9','lesson':'Lesson 6',     'focus':'LKS2 Word List',     'explanation':'This lesson focuses on the following words from the LKS2 Statutory Word List: imagine / medicine / notice / promise. These words all look like they end with a split digraph. However, the e is silent and the other vowel produces the short i sound. The word imagine contains the j sound spelled g. The word medicine contains the s sound spelled c. The word notice contains the s sound spelled c. The word promise contains the s sound. However, although it sounds like it ends with miss, there is only one s.',     'hlWords':['imagine', 'medicine', 'notice', 'promise', 'height', 'straight', 'eighth', 'eight', 'weight', 'though']},
    {'id':'y3-t6-w1-l1','year':'Y3','term':'T6',     'week':1,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 9','lesson':'Lesson 7',     'focus':'LKS2 Word List',     'explanation':'This lesson focuses on the following words from the LKS2 Statutory Word List: early / earth / learn / heart / guide / guard. Four of these words contain the ear grapheme. In the words early, earth and learn, the ear grapheme represents the er sound. In the word heart, the ear grapheme represents the ar sound. The final two words (guide / guard) contain a silent u after the letter g at the beginning of the word.',     'hlWords':['early', 'earth', 'guide', 'heart', 'guard', 'learn', 'build', 'answer', 'island', 'natural']},
    {'id':'y3-t6-w1-l2','year':'Y3','term':'T6',     'week':1,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 9','lesson':'Lesson 8',     'focus':'LKS2 Word List',     'explanation':'This lesson focuses on the following words from the LKS2 Statutory Word List: build / answer / island / often / interest / history / natural / knowledge. The focus of this lesson in on identifying the silent letter within each word. build (u) / answer (w) / island (s) / often (t) / interest (e) / natural (u). history (o) – This word contains the word ‘story’. knowledge (k/w) – This word contains two silent letters. The root word is ‘know’.',     'hlWords':['build', 'answer', 'island', 'natural', 'often', 'history', 'interest', 'knowledge', 'early', 'earth']},
    {'id':'y3-t6-w1-l3','year':'Y3','term':'T6',     'week':1,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 9','lesson':'Lesson 9',     'focus':'LKS2 Word List',     'explanation':'This lesson focuses on the following words from the LKS2 Statutory Word List: believe / business / favourite / February / library / forward. The focus of this lesson in on identifying the challenging parts of each word. believe – The long e sound is represented by the ie grapheme. The ‘be’ start often sounds like ‘bi’ when spoken. business – The root word is busy. We change the y to i before applying -ness. favourite – The word favourite contains the word ‘our’. It ends with a silent e. February – The letter r (after b) is often missed. The word needs capitalising because it is a proper noun. library – The word library ends in the -ary suffix. forward – This can be broken down into two words (for/ward). The second part (ward) sounds like ‘word’.',     'hlWords':['believe', 'business', 'favourite', 'February', 'library', 'forward', 'early', 'earth', 'guide', 'heart']},
    {'id':'y3-t6-w2-l1','year':'Y3','term':'T6',     'week':2,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Mastery 4','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'words ending -sion',     'explanation':'The -sion ending is applied to verbs to form nouns. The -sion ending sounds like zh-un. When a verb ends in se, we can remove se and replace it with sion. For example, the verb confuse can be used to form the noun confusion. When a verb ends in de, we can remove de and replace it with sion. For example, the verb decide can be used to form the noun decision.',     'hlWords':['decide', 'decision', 'diffuse', 'diffusion', 'televise', 'television', 'confusion', 'confuse', 'division', 'divide', 'precision', 'precise', 'conclusion', 'conclude', 'revision', 'revise', 'erosion', 'erode', 'explosion', 'explode', 'version', 'verse', 'invasion', 'invade', 'collision', 'collide', 'supervision', 'supervise']},
