    {'id':'y3-t6-w5-l1','year':'Y3','term':'T6',     'week':5,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Mastery 7','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'tion/sion/ssion/cian',     'explanation':'These suffixes are pronounced like ‘shun’. The -tion suffix is often used if the root word ends in t or te. For example, the word complete becomes completion. The -sion suffix is often used if the root word ends in d, de or se. For example, the word decide becomes decision. The -ssion suffix is often used if the root word ends in ss or mit. For example, the word admit becomes admission. The -cian suffix is often used if the root word ends in c or cs. For example, the word electric becomes electrician. It is mostly used to indicate a profession – e.g. magician. It is important to note that the ending of the root word (e.g. mit or cs) is removed before adding the suffix. Note that words ending in ‘mit’ technically end in ‘t’ so checking for the difference between ‘mit’ and ‘t’ is important.',     'hlWords':['elect', 'election', 'create', 'creation', 'adopt', 'adoption', 'pollute', 'pollution', 'select', 'selection', 'animate', 'animation', 'extend', 'extension', 'decide', 'decision', 'expand', 'expansion', 'include', 'inclusion', 'ascend', 'ascension', 'tense', 'tension', 'detect', 'detection', 'divide', 'division', 'migrate', 'migration', 'donate', 'educate', 'conclude', 'collect', 'donation', 'education', 'conclusion', 'collection', 'operate', 'invade', 'persuade', 'promote', 'operation', 'invasion', 'persuasion', 'promotion', 'invention']},
