    {'id':'y4-t1-w1-l1','year':'Y4','term':'T1',     'week':1,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'doubling consonants',     'explanation':"When a word with more than one syllable ends in a single short vowel letter + consonant, and the last syllable is stressed we double the consonant before adding the vowel suffix. (A syllable being stressed means that it is the most emphasised syllable – it is louder or clearer than the others when spoken aloud.) The word 'open' ends in a single vowel (e) + single consonant (n). In the word o-pen, it is the first syllable (making the long o sound) that is stressed. Therefore, when we add a vowel suffix, we do not double the final consonant. For example, we simply add the -ed suffix to get opened. The word 'begin' ends in a single vowel (i) + single consonant (n). In the word be-gin, it is the final syllable (gin) that is stressed. Therefore, when we add a vowel suffix, we DO double the final consonant. For example, when we add the -er suffix, we get beginner.",     'hlWords':['listening', 'referral', 'committee', 'respecting', 'beginner', 'responded', 'covering', 'preferring', 'referred', 'gardener', 'limited', 'inferred', 'forgetting']},
    {'id':'y4-t1-w1-l2','year':'Y4','term':'T1',     'week':1,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'doubling consonants',     'explanation':"When a word with more than one syllable ends in a single short vowel letter + consonant, and the last syllable is stressed we double the consonant before adding the vowel suffix. (A syllable being stressed means that it is the most emphasised syllable – it is louder or clearer than the others when spoken aloud.) The word 'open' ends in a single vowel (e) + single consonant (n). In the word o-pen, it is the first syllable (making the long o sound) that is stressed. Therefore, when we add a vowel suffix, we do not double the final consonant. For example, we simply add the -ed suffix to get opened. The word 'begin' ends in a single vowel (i) + single consonant (n). In the word be-gin, it is the final syllable (gin) that is stressed. Therefore, when we add a vowel suffix, we DO double the final consonant. For example, when we add the -er suffix, we get beginner.",     'hlWords':['focusing', 'transmitting', 'committed', 'regretted', 'covered', 'inferring', 'happening', 'occurrence', 'equipped', 'whispering', 'transferred', 'preferred']},
    {'id':'y4-t1-w1-l3','year':'Y4','term':'T1',     'week':1,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'doubling consonants',     'explanation':"When a word with more than one syllable ends in a single short vowel letter + consonant, and the last syllable is stressed we double the consonant before adding the vowel suffix. (A syllable being stressed means that it is the most emphasised syllable – it is louder or clearer than the others when spoken aloud.) The word 'open' ends in a single vowel (e) + single consonant (n). In the word o-pen, it is the first syllable (making the long o sound) that is stressed. Therefore, when we add a vowel suffix, we do not double the final consonant. For example, we simply add the -ed suffix to get opened. The word 'begin' ends in a single vowel (i) + single consonant (n). In the word be-gin, it is the final syllable (gin) that is stressed. Therefore, when we add a vowel suffix, we DO double the final consonant. For example, when we add the -er suffix, we get beginner.",     'hlWords':['preferred', 'preference', 'benefitting', 'controlled', 'occurred', 'admitting', 'forgotten', 'gardener', 'transferring', 'beginning', 'equipped']},
    {'id':'y4-t1-w2-l1','year':'Y4','term':'T1',     'week':2,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 4',     'focus':'adding the -ly suffix',     'explanation':'For most root words, we simply add the -ly suffix (because it begins with a consonant). However, there are some exceptions to this where other rules apply. If the root word ends in y, change y to i before adding -ly. For example, the word happy becomes happily. If the root word ends in -le, change -le to -ly. For example, the word gentle becomes gently. If the root word ends in -ic, use the -ally suffix (instead of -ly). For example, the word magic becomes magically.',     'hlWords':['possibly', 'magically', 'recently', 'steadily', 'easily', 'terribly', 'grumpily', 'basically', 'calmly', 'sensibly', 'simply', 'angrily', 'logically']},
    {'id':'y4-t1-w2-l2','year':'Y4','term':'T1',     'week':2,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 5',     'focus':'adding the -ly suffix',     'explanation':'For most root words, we simply add the -ly suffix (because it begins with a consonant). However, there are some exceptions to this where other rules apply. If the root word ends in y, change y to i before adding -ly. For example, the word happy becomes happily. If the root word ends in -le, change -le to -ly. For example, the word gentle becomes gently. If the root word ends in -ic, use the -ally suffix (instead of -ly). For example, the word magic becomes magically.',     'hlWords':['specifically', 'honestly', 'clumsily', 'legibly', 'quickly', 'luckily', 'gently', 'sleepily', 'tragically', 'bravely', 'musically', 'clearly', 'greedily']},
    {'id':'y4-t1-w2-l3','year':'Y4','term':'T1',     'week':2,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 6',     'focus':'adding the -ous suffix',     'explanation':"When there is an obvious root word, the usual rules apply for adding a vowel suffix. For most words, the -ous suffix is simply added. For example, the word danger becomes dangerous. When the root word ends in y, we change y to i before adding -ous. For example, the word vary becomes various. When the root word ends in e, we remove the e before adding -ous. For example, the word fame becomes famous. In addition, if the root word ends in -our, we change it to -or before adding -ous. For example, the word humour becomes humorous. The final 'e' of the root word must be kept if the /dʒ/ sound of 'g' is still present. For example, the word courage becomes courageous. If there is an /i:/ sound before the –ous ending, it is usually spelt as i, but a few words have e. Examples include serious, obvious and curious. An example of an exception is hideous.",     'hlWords':['glamorous', 'glorious', 'venomous', 'adventurous', 'dangerous', 'furious', 'nervous', 'outrageous', 'humorous', 'poisonous', 'courageous', 'mysterious', 'famous', 'envious']},
    {'id':'y4-t1-w3-l1','year':'Y4','term':'T1',     'week':3,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 7',     'focus':'the suffixes -tion/-sion/-ssion/-cian',     'explanation':"These suffixes are pronounced like 'shun'. The -tion suffix is often used if the root word ends in t or te. For example, the word complete becomes completion. The -sion suffix is often used if the root word ends in d, de or se. For example, the word decide becomes decision. The -ssion suffix is often used if the root word ends in ss or mit. For example, the word admit becomes admission. The -cian suffix is often used if the root word ends in c or cs. For example, the word electric becomes electrician. It is mostly used to indicate a profession – e.g. magician. It is important to note that the ending of the root word (e.g. mit or cs) is removed before adding the suffix. Note that words ending in 'mit' technically end in 't' so checking for the difference between 'mit' and 't' is important.",     'hlWords':['invasion', 'population', 'eruption', 'election', 'calculation', 'confusion', 'vacation', 'division', 'selection', 'creation', 'explosion', 'injection']},
    {'id':'y4-t1-w3-l2','year':'Y4','term':'T1',     'week':3,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 8',     'focus':'the suffixes -tion/-sion/-ssion/-cian',     'explanation':"These suffixes are pronounced like 'shun'. The -tion suffix is often used if the root word ends in t or te. For example, the word complete becomes completion. The -sion suffix is often used if the root word ends in d, de or se. For example, the word decide becomes decision. The -ssion suffix is often used if the root word ends in ss or mit. For example, the word admit becomes admission. The -cian suffix is often used if the root word ends in c or cs. For example, the word electric becomes electrician. It is mostly used to indicate a profession – e.g. magician. It is important to note that the ending of the root word (e.g. mit or cs) is removed before adding the suffix. Note that words ending in 'mit' technically end in 't' so checking for the difference between 'mit' and 't' is important.",     'hlWords':['provision', 'permission', 'opposition', 'interaction', 'admission', 'extension', 'invasion', 'expression', 'rotation', 'discussion', 'location', 'conclusion']},
    {'id':'y4-t1-w3-l3','year':'Y4','term':'T1',     'week':3,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 9',     'focus':'the suffixes -tion/-sion/-ssion/-cian',     'explanation':"These suffixes are pronounced like 'shun'. The -tion suffix is often used if the root word ends in t or te. For example, the word complete becomes completion. The -sion suffix is often used if the root word ends in d, de or se. For example, the word decide becomes decision. The -ssion suffix is often used if the root word ends in ss or mit. For example, the word admit becomes admission. The -cian suffix is often used if the root word ends in c or cs. For example, the word electric becomes electrician. It is mostly used to indicate a profession – e.g. magician. It is important to note that the ending of the root word (e.g. mit or cs) is removed before adding the suffix. Note that words ending in 'mit' technically end in 't' so checking for the difference between 'mit' and 't' is important.",     'hlWords':['physician', 'animation', 'omission', 'politician', 'permission', 'expansion', 'connection', 'musician', 'mathematician', 'magician', 'collection', 'electrician']},
    {'id':'y4-t1-w4-l1','year':'Y4','term':'T1',     'week':4,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 1','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'prefixes',     'explanation':"Prefixes are units of meaning added to the beginning of a root word in order to change its meaning. The un- prefix usually means 'not'. For example, the word unhappy means not happy. It can, in some cases, mean to 'reverse' something. For example, the word unlock means to reverse locking something. The dis- prefix usually means 'the opposite of' or 'not'. For example, the word disobey is the opposite of the word 'obey' and means to not obey. It can, in some cases, mean 'apart' or 'away'. For example, the word disappear is the opposite of appear – but it also shows that something has gone away. The mis- prefix means 'wrongly' or 'incorrectly'. For example, the word misread means to read something incorrectly. These prefixes are usually added with no change to the root word. Note that the prefixes dis- and mis- end in one consonant (s). It is a common misconception for children to spell these prefixes with a double consonant. However, when a root word begins with an s, this will lead to a double letter – for example, misspell.",     'hlWords':['unfair', 'unkind', 'unlock', 'unhappy', 'unknown', 'dislike', 'disobey', 'disloyal', 'disable', 'disagree', 'misuse', 'misread', 'mistrust', 'misjudge', 'mislead', 'disapprove', 'misbehave', 'unusual', 'unwell', 'disorder', 'miscount', 'mismatch', 'disappear', 'untrue', 'unclear']},
    {'id':'y4-t1-w4-l2','year':'Y4','term':'T1',     'week':4,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 1','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'prefixes',     'explanation':"Prefixes are units of meaning added to the beginning of a root word in order to change its meaning. The un- prefix usually means 'not'. For example, the word unhappy means not happy. It can, in some cases, mean to 'reverse' something. For example, the word unlock means to reverse locking something. The dis- prefix usually means 'the opposite of' or 'not'. For example, the word disobey is the opposite of the word 'obey' and means to not obey. It can, in some cases, mean 'apart' or 'away'. For example, the word disappear is the opposite of appear – but it also shows that something has gone away. The mis- prefix means 'wrongly' or 'incorrectly'. For example, the word misread means to read something incorrectly. These prefixes are usually added with no change to the root word. Note that the prefixes dis- and mis- end in one consonant (s). It is a common misconception for children to spell these prefixes with a double consonant. However, when a root word begins with an s, this will lead to a double letter – for example, misspell.",     'hlWords':['mistaken', 'unlikely', 'dishonest', 'unusual', 'disallow', 'uncommon', 'misguide', 'misread', 'disgrace', 'disconnect', 'mistreat', 'uncover']},
    {'id':'y4-t1-w4-l3','year':'Y4','term':'T1',     'week':4,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 1','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'prefixes',     'explanation':"Prefixes are units of meaning added to the beginning of a root word in order to change its meaning. The un- prefix usually means 'not'. For example, the word unhappy means not happy. It can, in some cases, mean to 'reverse' something. For example, the word unlock means to reverse locking something. The dis- prefix usually means 'the opposite of' or 'not'. For example, the word disobey is the opposite of the word 'obey' and means to not obey. It can, in some cases, mean 'apart' or 'away'. For example, the word disappear is the opposite of appear – but it also shows that something has gone away. The mis- prefix means 'wrongly' or 'incorrectly'. For example, the word misread means to read something incorrectly. These prefixes are usually added with no change to the root word. Note that the prefixes dis- and mis- end in one consonant (s). It is a common misconception for children to spell these prefixes with a double consonant. However, when a root word begins with an s, this will lead to a double letter – for example, misspell.",     'hlWords':['misshape', 'displeased', 'misspend', 'uncertain', 'unsure', 'mislabel', 'disloyal', 'misspeak', 'unpopular', 'disprove', 'misspell', 'disappear', 'untidy']},
    {'id':'y4-t1-w5-l1','year':'Y4','term':'T1',     'week':5,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 1','lesson':'Lesson 4',     'focus':'prefixes',     'explanation':"Prefixes are units of meaning added to the beginning of a root word in order to change its meaning. The prefixes in- / il- / im- / ir- all have the same meaning – they mean 'not'. The in- prefix is the most common. However, it is important to consider the root word when deciding which prefix to apply. The il- prefix is applied when the root word begins with the letter 'l'. For example, the word 'legal' becomes 'illegal'. The ir- prefix is applied when the root word begins with the letter 'r'. For example, the word 'rational' becomes 'irrational'. The im- prefix is applied when the root word begins with the letters 'm' or 'p'. For example, the words 'mature' and 'polite' become 'immature' and 'impolite'. These prefixes are usually added with no change to the root word. Note that applying these prefixes can sometimes lead to a double consonant. This is caused by the ending consonant of the prefix being the same as the beginning letter in the root word (e.g. illegal).",     'hlWords':['inactive', 'insecure', 'invisible', 'indirect', 'immature', 'immoral', 'impatient', 'impolite', 'illegal', 'illegible', 'illogical', 'illuminate', 'irregular', 'irrelevant', 'irrational', 'irrelative', 'impossible', 'irreligious', 'inconsistent', 'impersonal', 'incomplete', 'immobile']},
    {'id':'y4-t1-w5-l2','year':'Y4','term':'T1',     'week':5,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 1','lesson':'Lesson 5',     'focus':'prefixes',     'explanation':"Prefixes are units of meaning added to the beginning of a root word in order to change its meaning. The prefixes in- / il- / im- / ir- all have the same meaning – they mean 'not'. The in- prefix is the most common. However, it is important to consider the root word when deciding which prefix to apply. The il- prefix is applied when the root word begins with the letter 'l'. For example, the word 'legal' becomes 'illegal'. The ir- prefix is applied when the root word begins with the letter 'r'. For example, the word 'rational' becomes 'irrational'. The im- prefix is applied when the root word begins with the letters 'm' or 'p'. For example, the words 'mature' and 'polite' become 'immature' and 'impolite'. These prefixes are usually added with no change to the root word. Note that applying these prefixes can sometimes lead to a double consonant. This is caused by the ending consonant of the prefix being the same as the beginning letter in the root word (e.g. illegal).",     'hlWords':['insensitive', 'irrational', 'imprecise', 'impatient', 'illegible', 'inaccurate', 'irresponsible', 'impolite', 'infrequent', 'incomplete', 'impossible']},
