    {'id':'y4-t3-w1-l1','year':'Y4','term':'T3',     'week':1,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 4','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'LKS2 Word List',     'explanation':'This sequence focuses on the following words from the LKS2 Statutory Word List: accident, address, appear, arrive, different, difficult, disappear, grammar, occasion, opposite, possess, possible, pressure, suppose. These words all contain double consonants. Two of the words (address / possess) contain two pairs of double consonants. A double consonant usually follows a short vowel sound. However, not all short vowel sounds are followed by a double consonant so identifying where the double consonant goes is important. In some words (e.g. different), there is a suffix (e.g. ent). In this case, the consonant n is not doubled because, although it follows a short vowel sound (e), it forms part of a suffix. It is important throughout this sequence that children are frequently asked to read the words aloud.',     'hlWords':['accident', 'occasion', 'difficult', 'appear', 'different', 'address', 'possible', 'suppose', 'arrive', 'opposite', 'possess', 'grammar']},
    {'id':'y4-t3-w1-l2','year':'Y4','term':'T3',     'week':1,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 4','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'LKS2 Word List',     'explanation':'This sequence focuses on the following words from the LKS2 Statutory Word List: accident, address, appear, arrive, different, difficult, disappear, grammar, occasion, opposite, possess, possible, pressure, suppose. These words all contain double consonants. Two of the words (address / possess) contain two pairs of double consonants. A double consonant usually follows a short vowel sound. However, not all short vowel sounds are followed by a double consonant so identifying where the double consonant goes is important. In some words (e.g. different), there is a suffix (e.g. ent). In this case, the consonant n is not doubled because, although it follows a short vowel sound (e), it forms part of a suffix. It is important throughout this sequence that children are frequently asked to read the words aloud.',     'hlWords':['occasion', 'difficult', 'possess', 'grammar', 'opposite', 'suppose', 'different', 'disappear', 'appear', 'certain']},
    {'id':'y4-t3-w1-l3','year':'Y4','term':'T3',     'week':1,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 4','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'LKS2 Word List',     'explanation':'This sequence focuses on the following words from the LKS2 Statutory Word List: accident, address, appear, arrive, different, difficult, disappear, grammar, occasion, opposite, possess, possible, pressure, suppose. These words all contain double consonants. Two of the words (address / possess) contain two pairs of double consonants. A double consonant usually follows a short vowel sound. However, not all short vowel sounds are followed by a double consonant so identifying where the double consonant goes is important. In some words (e.g. different), there is a suffix (e.g. ent). In this case, the consonant n is not doubled because, although it follows a short vowel sound (e), it forms part of a suffix. It is important throughout this sequence that children are frequently asked to read the words aloud.',     'hlWords':['accident', 'disappear', 'arrive', 'pressure', 'different', 'address', 'possess', 'appear', 'occasion', 'opposite', 'difficult', 'possible', 'suppose']},
    {'id':'y4-t3-w2-l1','year':'Y4','term':'T3',     'week':2,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 4','lesson':'Lesson 4',     'focus':'LKS2 Word List',     'explanation':'This sequence focuses on the following words from the LKS2 Statutory Word List: consider, quarter, remember, particular, peculiar, popular, regular, calendar, grammar, centre. perhaps, purpose, surprise, circle, certain, exercise. These words all contain the er grapheme and its alternative spellings (ar/ur/ir/re). The first set contain these graphemes at the end of each word. The second set contain these graphemes near the beginning of each word. Most often, words end in the er grapheme. When a word has Latin or French origins, it often uses the ar grapheme. Most of the words ending in this grapheme are adjectives (particular, peculiar, popular, regular). The re grapheme is extremely rare and is the British spelling (e.g. centre) for words spelled with er in American (center). It is important throughout this sequence that children are frequently asked to read the words aloud.',     'hlWords':['quarter', 'remember', 'particular', 'peculiar', 'calendar', 'regular', 'popular', 'consider', 'certain', 'circle']},
