    {'id':'y4-t3-w3-l3','year':'Y4','term':'T3',     'week':3,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 4','lesson':'Lesson 9',     'focus':'LKS2 Word List',     'explanation':'This sequence focuses on the following words from the LKS2 Statutory Word List that contain alternative spellings: bicycle, centre, century, certain, circle, decide, exercise, recent (s spelled c). busy, exercise (z spelled s). fruit, group (alternative oo sound). increase (alternative long e spelled ea). material (alternative ear sound spelled er). minute (short i sound spelled u). therefore (alternative air sound spelled ere + silent e). potatoes (alternative long o spelled oe – this is because the root word ends in consonant + o so we add -es to form the plural). It is important throughout this sequence that children are frequently asked to read the words aloud.',     'hlWords':['certain', 'material', 'fruit', 'century', 'minute', 'centre', 'increase', 'therefore', 'recent', 'decide', 'circle', 'exercise']},
    {'id':'y4-t3-w4-l1','year':'Y4','term':'T3',     'week':4,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Mastery 1','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'prefixes',     'explanation':"Prefixes are units of meaning added to the beginning of a root word in order to change its meaning. The un- prefix usually means 'not' (as in unhappy) or 'to reverse' (as in unlock). The dis- prefix usually means 'the opposite of' or 'not' (as in disobey meaning to not obey). The mis- prefix means 'wrongly' or 'incorrectly' (as in misread meaning to read something incorrectly.) The prefixes in- / il- / im- / ir- all have the same meaning – they mean 'not' (with in- being the most commonly used). The il- prefix is applied when the root word begins with the letter 'l'. For example, the word 'legal' becomes 'illegal'. The ir- prefix is applied when the root word begins with the letter 'r'. For example, the word 'rational' becomes 'irrational'. The im- prefix is applied when the root word begins with the letters 'm' or 'p'. For example, the words 'mature' and 'polite' become 'immature' and 'impolite'. These prefixes are usually added with no change to the root word. Note that the prefixes dis- and mis- end in one consonant (s). With all of these prefixes, a double letter can be caused when the final consonant of the prefix and the first letter of the root word are the same (e.g. misspelled or immature).",     'hlWords':['unhappy', 'unknown', 'unlikely', 'disobey', 'disappear', 'disagree', 'misread', 'mistake', 'misspell', 'uncommon', 'disqualify', 'uncertain', 'unequal', 'misprint', 'unhelpful', 'mistreat', 'discount', 'miscount', 'unexpected', 'discomfort', 'unclear', 'disorder']},
