    {'id':'y4-t3-w4-l3','year':'Y4','term':'T3',     'week':4,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Mastery 1','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'prefixes',     'explanation':"Prefixes are units of meaning added to the beginning of a root word in order to change its meaning. The un- prefix usually means 'not' (as in unhappy) or 'to reverse' (as in unlock). The dis- prefix usually means 'the opposite of' or 'not' (as in disobey meaning to not obey). The mis- prefix means 'wrongly' or 'incorrectly' (as in misread meaning to read something incorrectly.) The prefixes in- / il- / im- / ir- all have the same meaning – they mean 'not' (with in- being the most commonly used). The il- prefix is applied when the root word begins with the letter 'l'. For example, the word 'legal' becomes 'illegal'. The ir- prefix is applied when the root word begins with the letter 'r'. For example, the word 'rational' becomes 'irrational'. The im- prefix is applied when the root word begins with the letters 'm' or 'p'. For example, the words 'mature' and 'polite' become 'immature' and 'impolite'. These prefixes are usually added with no change to the root word. Note that the prefixes dis- and mis- end in one consonant (s). With all of these prefixes, a double letter can be caused when the final consonant of the prefix and the first letter of the root word are the same (e.g. misspelled or immature).",     'hlWords':['uncertain', 'misspell', 'mistreat', 'discomfort', 'unexpected', 'disallow', 'impatient', 'invisible', 'illegible', 'irregular', 'impossible', 'immature', 'misread', 'unequal', 'imperfect', 'irrational', 'uncommon', 'irrelevant']},
    {'id':'y4-t3-w5-l1','year':'Y4','term':'T3',     'week':5,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Mastery 2','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'words ending sure/ture',     'explanation':"The -sure ending sounds like /ʒə/ (which sounds like zhuh). This means that it makes a soft 'z' sound (ʒ) followed by the schwa (ə) that sounds like 'uh'. Examples of words ending in -sure include measure, treasure, leisure and enclosure. The -ture ending sounds like /tʃə/ (which sounds like chuh). This means that it makes the 'ch' sound (tʃ) followed by the schwa (ə) that sounds like 'uh'. Examples of words ending in –ture include picture, adventure, structure and creature. There is not a clear rule to follow for these endings – but the sounds they form are clearly different so oral pronunciation is important. However, often, if the letter before the ending is a consonant, the ending applied is -ture. When a word contains the 'ea' grapheme before the ending, and it makes a short vowel sound, often the -sure ending is applied. Similarly, when a word contains the 'ea' grapheme before the ending, and it makes a long vowel sound, often the -ture ending is applied. Words ending in -ture can be confused with words that end in (t)ch + er. The 'cher' or 'tcher' ending happens when there is a root word ending in -ch (teach) or -tch (watch). These words can have the -er suffix applied, resulting in teacher or watcher. The resulting word (e.g. teacher) sounds the same as the -ture ending.",     'hlWords':['lecture', 'exposure', 'departure', 'measure', 'structure', 'leisure', 'closure', 'creature', 'furniture', 'culture', 'adventure', 'treasure', 'enclosure', 'fracture']},
    {'id':'y4-t3-w5-l2','year':'Y4','term':'T3',     'week':5,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Mastery 2','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'words ending sure/ture',     'explanation':"The -sure ending sounds like /ʒə/ (which sounds like zhuh). This means that it makes a soft 'z' sound (ʒ) followed by the schwa (ə) that sounds like 'uh'. Examples of words ending in -sure include measure, treasure, leisure and enclosure. The -ture ending sounds like /tʃə/ (which sounds like chuh). This means that it makes the 'ch' sound (tʃ) followed by the schwa (ə) that sounds like 'uh'. Examples of words ending in –ture include picture, adventure, structure and creature. There is not a clear rule to follow for these endings – but the sounds they form are clearly different so oral pronunciation is important. However, often, if the letter before the ending is a consonant, the ending applied is -ture. When a word contains the 'ea' grapheme before the ending, and it makes a short vowel sound, often the -sure ending is applied. Similarly, when a word contains the 'ea' grapheme before the ending, and it makes a long vowel sound, often the -ture ending is applied. Words ending in -ture can be confused with words that end in (t)ch + er. The 'cher' or 'tcher' ending happens when there is a root word ending in -ch (teach) or -tch (watch). These words can have the -er suffix applied, resulting in teacher or watcher. The resulting word (e.g. teacher) sounds the same as the -ture ending.",     'hlWords':['departure', 'treasure', 'structure', 'sculpture', 'mixture', 'composure', 'creature', 'sketcher', 'poacher', 'teacher', 'feature', 'butcher', 'archer', 'catcher', 'culture', 'adventure', 'preacher', 'stretcher', 'pitcher', 'capture', 'fracture', 'richer']},
    {'id':'y4-t3-w5-l3','year':'Y4','term':'T3',     'week':5,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Mastery 2','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'words ending sure/ture',     'explanation':"The -sure ending sounds like /ʒə/ (which sounds like zhuh). This means that it makes a soft 'z' sound (ʒ) followed by the schwa (ə) that sounds like 'uh'. Examples of words ending in -sure include measure, treasure, leisure and enclosure. The -ture ending sounds like /tʃə/ (which sounds like chuh). This means that it makes the 'ch' sound (tʃ) followed by the schwa (ə) that sounds like 'uh'. Examples of words ending in –ture include picture, adventure, structure and creature. There is not a clear rule to follow for these endings – but the sounds they form are clearly different so oral pronunciation is important. However, often, if the letter before the ending is a consonant, the ending applied is -ture. When a word contains the 'ea' grapheme before the ending, and it makes a short vowel sound, often the -sure ending is applied. Similarly, when a word contains the 'ea' grapheme before the ending, and it makes a long vowel sound, often the -ture ending is applied. Words ending in -ture can be confused with words that end in (t)ch + er. The 'cher' or 'tcher' ending happens when there is a root word ending in -ch (teach) or -tch (watch). These words can have the -er suffix applied, resulting in teacher or watcher. The resulting word (e.g. teacher) sounds the same as the -ture ending.",     'hlWords':['teacher', 'culture', 'measure', 'sketcher', 'leisure', 'creature', 'texture', 'structure', 'closure', 'sculpture', 'voucher', 'butcher', 'richer', 'enclosure', 'picture', 'capture', 'furniture', 'mixture', 'preacher', 'moisture', 'adventure', 'departure', 'stretcher', 'literature']},
    {'id':'y4-t4-w1-l1','year':'Y4','term':'T4',     'week':1,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 4','lesson':'Lesson 10',     'focus':'LKS2 Word List',     'explanation':"This lesson focuses on the following words from the LKS2 Statutory Word List: actually, occasionally, accidentally, probably, possible. The focus of this lesson is on words ending in -ly. It makes links to children's prior knowledge that, when a root word ends in -le, we change -le to -ly. Examples of this include probable becoming probably and possible becoming possibly. Where -ally is used, there is a word ending in -al with the -ly suffix then applied. Actual becomes actually. Occasional becomes occasionally. Accidental becomes accidentally. It is important throughout this lesson that children are frequently asked to read the words aloud. It is also important that children are encouraged to identify and discuss elements of each word that are more challenging to spell. For example, in probably, the pro- and -ly parts sound how they are spelled – the tricky part of this word is in the middle (bab).",     'hlWords':['possible', 'probably', 'actually', 'occasionally', 'accidentally', 'possession', 'question', 'mention', 'position', 'various']},
    {'id':'y4-t4-w1-l2','year':'Y4','term':'T4',     'week':1,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 4','lesson':'Lesson 11',     'focus':'LKS2 Word List',     'explanation':"This lesson focuses on the following words from the LKS2 Statutory Word List: mention, position, question, possession, famous, various. These words all contain a -tion ending (including possession with the alternative -ssion) or the -ous suffix. The -tion ending sounds like 'shun'. In most cases, it is spelled -tion. For some words (like possession), an alternative spelling is applied due to the spelling of the root word. For the words ending in -ous, the usual rules for applying a vowel suffix apply. When the root word ends in e (fame), remove the e before adding -ous (famous). When the root word ends in y (vary), change y to i before adding -ous (various). It is important throughout this lesson that children are frequently asked to read the words aloud.",     'hlWords':['possession', 'question', 'mention', 'position', 'various', 'famous', 'possible', 'probably', 'actually', 'occasionally']},
    {'id':'y4-t4-w1-l3','year':'Y4','term':'T4',     'week':1,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 4','lesson':'Lesson 12',     'focus':'LKS2 Word List',     'explanation':'This lesson focuses on the following words from the LKS2 Statutory Word List: sentence, experience, experiment, important, special, ordinary. In this lesson, the words all end in common suffixes – often with tricky spelling patterns or rules. The focus is on children identifying similarities and challenging parts in the words. For example, the words experience and experiment both contain the spelling pattern experi and the words experience and sentence both end in -ence. Many of the words end in ence/ent/ant. The word special ends in the shul sound spelled cial. The word ordinary ends in the air-ee sound spelled ary. It is important throughout this lesson that children are frequently asked to read the words aloud.',     'hlWords':['experience', 'experiment', 'sentence', 'special', 'important', 'ordinary', 'possible', 'probably', 'actually', 'occasionally']},
    {'id':'y4-t4-w2-l1','year':'Y4','term':'T4',     'week':2,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 5','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'Greek & French sounds',     'explanation':'This sequence focuses on alternative spellings with Greek and French origins. There are no clear rules for these words. Instead, they have to be learned. k sound spelled ch. The k sound spelled ch has Greek origins. It is often used in school-related words, particularly those linked to the sciences and music. sh sound spelled ch. The sh sound spelled ch has French origins. s sound spelled sc. The s sound spelled sc has Greek (or Latin) origins. This sound is often found before the letters e, i or y.',     'hlWords':['chapter', 'cheese', 'teacher', 'chicken', 'chorus', 'stomach', 'chemist', 'echo', 'monarch', 'character', 'school', 'chaos', 'orchestra', 'orchid']},
