    {'id':'y4-t5-w1-l1','year':'Y4','term':'T5',     'week':1,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 7','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'possessive apostrophes',     'explanation':"Apostrophes can be used for two reasons: contractions and possession. When we use apostrophes for possession, we need to consider who the 'owner' is. I borrowed the teacher's book. In this sentence, the book is 'owned' by the teacher. Therefore, we place the apostrophe in the word teacher's. The apostrophe is placed before the letter 's' to show that one person has possession – this is known as singular possession. I borrowed the teachers' books. In this sentence, the books are 'owned' by multiple teachers. The apostrophe is placed after the letter 's'. This shows that more than one person has possession – this is known as plural possession. When a word is already a plural, like the word children, the apostrophe is placed before the s (children's). They attended the boy's performance. (one boy – before the s) They attended the boys' performance. (more than one boy – after the s) They attended the children's performance. (already plural – before the s)",     'hlWords':['books', "cat's", "boy's", "phone's", "child's", "author's", "tree's", 'cats', "man's", "friend's", "girl's", 'birds', "teacher's", "chef's", "flower's"]},
    {'id':'y4-t5-w1-l2','year':'Y4','term':'T5',     'week':1,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 7','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'possessive apostrophes',     'explanation':"Apostrophes can be used for two reasons: contractions and possession. When we use apostrophes for possession, we need to consider who the 'owner' is. I borrowed the teacher's book. In this sentence, the book is 'owned' by the teacher. Therefore, we place the apostrophe in the word teacher's. The apostrophe is placed before the letter 's' to show that one person has possession – this is known as singular possession. I borrowed the teachers' books. In this sentence, the books are 'owned' by multiple teachers. The apostrophe is placed after the letter 's'. This shows that more than one person has possession – this is known as plural possession. When a word is already a plural, like the word children, the apostrophe is placed before the s (children's). They attended the boy's performance. (one boy – before the s) They attended the boys' performance. (more than one boy – after the s) They attended the children's performance. (already plural – before the s)",     'hlWords':["sun's", "authors'", "boys'", "artist's", "drivers'", "visitors'", "chef's", "teachers'", "builder's", "students'", "farmers'"]},
