    {'id':'y5-t2-w1-l1','year':'Y5','term':'T2',     'week':1,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 2','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'words ending in -cial/-tial',     'explanation':'The suffix -cial is often applied after a vowel letter.\nFor example, the cial suffix at the end of the word official follows the vowel letter i.\nThe suffix -tial is often applied after a consonant letter.\nFor example, the tial suffix at the end of the word essential follows the consonant letter n.\nThere are some exceptions to this rule:\ninitial\nfinancial\nprovincial\ncommercial\nAlthough three of these words have root words ending in ce, and use the cial suffix, this is not a rule to follow. This is because other words, such as sequential also have root words ending in ce – sequence.\nTherefore, these exception words simply have to be learned.',     'hlWords':['judicial', 'presidential', 'consequential', 'special', 'essential', 'impartial', 'glacial', 'torrential', 'antisocial', 'martial', 'potential', 'facial']},
