    {'id':'y5-t2-w3-l1','year':'Y5','term':'T2',     'week':3,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 3','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'doubling consonants',     'explanation':"When a word with more than one syllable ends in a single short vowel letter + consonant, and the last syllable is stressed we double the consonant before adding the vowel suffix.\n(A syllable being stressed means that it is the most emphasised syllable – it is louder or clearer than the others when spoken aloud.)\nThe word 'open' ends in a single vowel (e) + single consonant (n).\nIn the word o-pen, it is the first syllable (making the long o sound) that is stressed.\nTherefore, when we add a vowel suffix, we do not double the final consonant (e.g. opened).\nThe word 'begin' ends in a single vowel (i) + single consonant (n).\nIn the word be-gin, it is the final syllable (gin) that is stressed.\nTherefore, when we add a vowel suffix, we DO double the final consonant (e.g. beginner).\nThe same rules apply for root words ending in -fer.\nNote that it is important to consider the whole word (including the suffix).\nFor example, when we add the -ence suffix to the root word prefer, we do not double the consonant (preference).\nHowever, when we add the -ed suffix to the root word prefer, we DO double the consonant (preferred).",     'hlWords':['happening', 'entered', 'opening', 'visited', 'limited', 'listened', 'entering', 'gardening', 'following', 'gardener', 'visiting', 'happened']},
    {'id':'y5-t2-w3-l2','year':'Y5','term':'T2',     'week':3,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 3','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'doubling consonants',     'explanation':"When a word with more than one syllable ends in a single short vowel letter + consonant, and the last syllable is stressed we double the consonant before adding the vowel suffix.\n(A syllable being stressed means that it is the most emphasised syllable – it is louder or clearer than the others when spoken aloud.)\nThe word 'open' ends in a single vowel (e) + single consonant (n).\nIn the word o-pen, it is the first syllable (making the long o sound) that is stressed.\nTherefore, when we add a vowel suffix, we do not double the final consonant (e.g. opened).\nThe word 'begin' ends in a single vowel (i) + single consonant (n).\nIn the word be-gin, it is the final syllable (gin) that is stressed.\nTherefore, when we add a vowel suffix, we DO double the final consonant (e.g. beginner).\nThe same rules apply for root words ending in -fer.\nNote that it is important to consider the whole word (including the suffix).\nFor example, when we add the -ence suffix to the root word prefer, we do not double the consonant (preference).\nHowever, when we add the -ed suffix to the root word prefer, we DO double the consonant (preferred).",     'hlWords':['permitted', 'occurring', 'controller', 'admitted', 'controlled', 'expelled', 'permitting', 'recurring', 'committing', 'beginning', 'regretted', 'forgetting']},
