    {'id':'y5-t5-w1-l1','year':'Y5','term':'T5',     'week':1,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 7','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':"Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings.\nyour / you're\nThe word your is possessive. It indicates that something belongs to you.\nPlease hand in your book.\nThe word you're is a contraction of you are.\nI know that you're trying hard.",     'hlWords':["you're", 'your', 'there', 'their', "they're", 'our', 'are', 'hour', 'father', 'further']},
    {'id':'y5-t5-w1-l2','year':'Y5','term':'T5',     'week':1,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 7','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':"Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings.\nthere / their / they're\nThe word there refers to a place or can be used to point out something.\nPut your shoes over there.\nThe word their shows possession. It indicates that someone owns something.\nWe visited their house yesterday.\nThe word they're is a contraction of they are.\nThey told me they're going to be late.",     'hlWords':['there', 'their', "they're", "you're", 'your', 'our', 'are', 'hour', 'father', 'further']},
    {'id':'y5-t5-w1-l3','year':'Y5','term':'T5',     'week':1,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 7','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':'Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings.\nour / are / hour\nThe word our is a possessive pronoun, which shows that something belongs to us.\nThis is our classroom.\nThe word are is a verb, which shows that someone is doing or being something.\nThey are working. We are happy today.\nThe word hour is a noun, referring to a length of time equal to 60 minutes.\nIn one hour, we will arrive at school.',     'hlWords':['our', 'are', 'hour', "you're", 'your', 'there', 'their', "they're", 'father', 'further']},
    {'id':'y5-t5-w2-l1','year':'Y5','term':'T5',     'week':2,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 7','lesson':'Lesson 4',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':'Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings.\nfather / further / farther\nThe word father refers to a person with a child.\nThe father held his baby.\nThe word further means more.\nI need to think about that further before I decide.\nThe word farther refers to a longer distance.\nTheir house is farther down the road.',     'hlWords':['father', 'further', 'farther', "you're", 'your', 'there', 'their', "they're", 'our', 'are']},
    {'id':'y5-t5-w2-l2','year':'Y5','term':'T5',     'week':2,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 7','lesson':'Lesson 5',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':'Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings.\nheard / herd\nThe word heard is a verb. It is the past tense of hear.\nI heard a loud noise outside.\nThe word herd is a collective noun. It refers to a group of animals (e.g. cows or elephants).\nIn the field, there was a herd of cows.',     'hlWords':['herd', 'heard', "you're", 'your', 'there', 'their', "they're", 'our', 'are', 'hour']},
    {'id':'y5-t5-w2-l3','year':'Y5','term':'T5',     'week':2,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 7','lesson':'Lesson 6',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':"Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings.\nsteal / steel\nThe word steal is a verb. It means to take something that you shouldn't take.\nIt is wrong to steal from someone else.\nThe word steel is a noun. It refers to a type of hard, grey metal.\nThe bars were made from steel.",     'hlWords':['steel', 'steal', "you're", 'your', 'there', 'their', "they're", 'our', 'are', 'hour']},
    {'id':'y5-t5-w3-l1','year':'Y5','term':'T5',     'week':3,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 7','lesson':'Lesson 7',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':"Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings.\nIn this unit, homophones ending with the letter t are nouns.\nTheir corresponding homophones ending with the -ed suffix are past tense verbs.\nguest / guessed\nThe word guest (ending with the letter t) is a noun. It refers to a person who is invited to or visits somewhere.\nWe have a special guest visiting tonight. The guest checked into the hotel.\nThe word guessed (ending with the -ed suffix) is a past tense verb. It means you tried to answer something when you weren't sure.\nI guessed the answer to the question.",     'hlWords':['guest', 'guessed', "you're", 'your', 'there', 'their', "they're", 'our', 'are', 'hour']},
    {'id':'y5-t5-w3-l2','year':'Y5','term':'T5',     'week':3,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 7','lesson':'Lesson 8',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':'Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings.\nIn this unit, homophones ending with the letter t are nouns.\nTheir corresponding homophones ending with the -ed suffix are past tense verbs.\npast / passed\nThe word past (ending with the letter t) is a noun. It refers to a time in history or something that is behind you.\nThat event happened in the past. We walked past the shop.\nThe word passed (ending with the -ed suffix) is a past tense verb. It means you went, gave or succeeded.\nI passed the shop. I passed the ball to my friend. I passed my test.',     'hlWords':['past', 'passed', "you're", 'your', 'there', 'their', "they're", 'our', 'are', 'hour']},
    {'id':'y5-t5-w3-l3','year':'Y5','term':'T5',     'week':3,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 7','lesson':'Lesson 9',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':"Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings.\nIn this unit, homophones ending with the letter t are nouns.\nTheir corresponding homophones ending with the -ed suffix are past tense verbs.\nmist / missed\nThe word mist (ending with the letter t) is a noun. It refers to light fog.\nMist covered the hills. The trees were surrounded by mist.\nThe word missed (ending with the -ed suffix) is a past tense verb. It means you didn't catch, hit or attend something.\nI missed the bus. She threw the ball but I missed it. At the weekend, I missed the show because I was ill.",     'hlWords':['mist', 'missed', "you're", 'your', 'there', 'their', "they're", 'our', 'are', 'hour']},
    {'id':'y5-t5-w4-l1','year':'Y5','term':'T5',     'week':4,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 8','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this sequence, children study words from the UKS2 statutory word list containing double consonants.\nIn this lesson, the words contain a double consonant after the letter a at the beginning of a word.\naccompany / according / apparent / appreciate / attached\nThese words do not follow clear rules and often contain exceptions to rules.\nThe focus of this sequence (in addition to identifying double consonants) is to identify trickier parts of each spelling and link the words to their meanings.\nFor example, in the word apparent, we can see the word rent. The -ent suffix is used (instead of -ant) even though it doesn't follow a soft c, soft g or qu grapheme.\nAnother example is the word appreciate. In the middle of the word, we can hear the sh sound. However, in this word, it is represented by the letter c.\nMore regular practice of these words in the children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['accompany', 'attached', 'according', 'apparent', 'appreciate', 'community', 'communicate', 'suggest', 'interrupt', 'marvellous']},
    {'id':'y5-t5-w4-l2','year':'Y5','term':'T5',     'week':4,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 8','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this sequence, children study words from the UKS2 statutory word list containing double consonants.\nIn this lesson, the words contain a double m after a short vowel sound.\ncommunicate / community / immediate / programme / recommend\nThese words do not follow clear rules and often contain exceptions to rules.\nThe focus of this sequence (in addition to identifying double consonants) is to identify trickier parts of each spelling and link the words to their meanings.\nFor example, the word programme ends with a silent e.\nIt is not part of a split digraph.\nAnother example is the word immediate. In the middle of the word, we can hear the long e sound. However, in this word, it is represented by the letter i.\nMore regular practice of these words in the children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['community', 'recommend', 'communicate', 'programme', 'immediate', 'suggest', 'interrupt', 'marvellous', 'exaggerate', 'harass']},
    {'id':'y5-t5-w4-l3','year':'Y5','term':'T5',     'week':4,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 8','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this sequence, children study words from the UKS2 statutory word list containing double consonants.\nIn this lesson, the words contain a double consonant after the short o sound.\ncommunicate / community / correspond / occupy / occur / opportunity\nThese words do not follow clear rules and often contain exceptions to rules.\nThe focus of this sequence (in addition to identifying double consonants) is to identify trickier parts of each spelling and link the words to their meanings.\nFor example, the word occur ends with the er sound spelled ur.\nIn addition, the word correspond contains another short o sound that is not followed by a double consonant. However, it helps to identify the word pond to help children remember this part of the word.\nMore regular practice of these words in the children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['occupy', 'community', 'occur', 'correspond', 'communicate', 'opportunity', 'suggest', 'interrupt', 'marvellous', 'exaggerate']},
    {'id':'y5-t5-w5-l1','year':'Y5','term':'T5',     'week':5,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 8','lesson':'Lesson 4',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this sequence, children study words from the UKS2 statutory word list containing double consonants.\nIn this lesson, the words contain two sets of double consonants.\naccommodate / aggressive / committee / embarrass\nNote that the word committee also contains a double vowel (ee).\nThese words do not follow clear rules and often contain exceptions to rules.\nThe focus of this sequence (in addition to identifying double consonants) is to identify trickier parts of each spelling and link the words to their meanings.\nFor example, the words accommodate and aggressive start with a short a – but pronunciation can make this sound like uh.\nMore regular practice of these words in the children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['committee', 'accommodate', 'aggressive', 'embarrass', 'community', 'communicate', 'suggest', 'interrupt', 'marvellous', 'exaggerate']},
    {'id':'y5-t5-w5-l2','year':'Y5','term':'T5',     'week':5,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 8','lesson':'Lesson 5',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this sequence, children study words from the UKS2 statutory word list containing double consonants.\nIn most of these words, the double consonant follows a short vowel sound.\nexaggerate / harass / interrupt / marvellous / necessary / sufficient / suggest\nNote that the exception is interrupt, which contains the er sound (instead of a short vowel).\nThese words do not follow clear rules and often contain exceptions to rules.\nThe focus of this sequence (in addition to identifying double consonants) is to identify trickier parts of each spelling and link the words to their meanings.\nFor example, the word marvellous ends in the -ous suffix (which sounds like us).\nAdditionally, the word necessary contains a soft c (producing the s sound).\nMore regular practice of these words in the children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['suggest', 'interrupt', 'marvellous', 'exaggerate', 'harass', 'necessary', 'sufficient', 'community', 'communicate', 'accompany']},
    {'id':'y5-t5-w5-l3','year':'Y5','term':'T5',     'week':5,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 8','lesson':'Lesson 6',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this sequence, children study words from the UKS2 statutory word list containing double consonants.\nIn most of these words, the double consonant follows a short vowel sound.\nexaggerate / harass / interrupt / marvellous / necessary / sufficient / suggest\nNote that the exception is interrupt, which contains the er sound (instead of a short vowel).\nThese words do not follow clear rules and often contain exceptions to rules.\nThe focus of this sequence (in addition to identifying double consonants) is to identify trickier parts of each spelling and link the words to their meanings.\nFor example, the word marvellous ends in the -ous suffix (which sounds like us).\nAdditionally, the word necessary contains a soft c (producing the s sound).\nMore regular practice of these words in the children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['suggest', 'harass', 'necessary', 'interrupt', 'exaggerate', 'sufficient', 'marvellous', 'community', 'communicate', 'accompany']},
    {'id':'y5-t6-w1-l1','year':'Y5','term':'T6',     'week':1,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 8','lesson':'Lesson 7',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this sequence, children study words from the UKS2 statutory word list.\nThe focus of this lesson is on words containing silent letters.\nrhyme / rhythm / twelfth / vehicle / guarantee\nThese words do not follow clear rules and simply have to be learned.\nHowever, spotting patterns, like linking the words twelve and twelfth can be helpful.\nMore regular practice of these words in children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['rhyme', 'rhythm', 'twelfth', 'guarantee', 'vehicle', 'leisure', 'shoulder', 'bargain', 'category', 'cemetery']},
    {'id':'y5-t6-w1-l2','year':'Y5','term':'T6',     'week':1,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 8','lesson':'Lesson 8',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this sequence, children study words from the UKS2 statutory word list.\nThe focus of this lesson is on words containing silent letters – or letters that are 'dropped' when spoken aloud.\nbargain / leisure / shoulder / category / cemetery\nThese words do not follow clear rules and simply have to be learned.\nHowever, spotting patterns, like breaking the word bargain into bar and gain can be helpful.\nMore regular practice of these words in children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['leisure', 'shoulder', 'bargain', 'category', 'cemetery', 'rhyme', 'rhythm', 'twelfth', 'guarantee', 'vehicle']},
    {'id':'y5-t6-w1-l3','year':'Y5','term':'T6',     'week':1,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 8','lesson':'Lesson 9',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this sequence, children study words from the UKS2 statutory word list.\nThe focus of this lesson is on words containing alternative sounds / graphemes.\nthorough / neighbour / symbol / muscle / physical\nThese words do not follow clear rules and simply have to be learned.\nthorough – The uh sound is represented by the letter o and the ough quadgraph.\nneighbour – The long a sound is represented by the eigh quadgraph. The er sound is represented by the our grapheme.\nsymbol – The short i sound is represented by the letter y. The le ending is represented by the ol grapheme.\nmuscle – The s sound is represented by the sc grapheme.\nphysical – The f sound is represented by the ph grapheme. The first short i sound is represented by the letter y. The le ending is represented by the alternative al grapheme.\nMore regular practice of these words in children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['muscle', 'symbol', 'physical', 'neighbour', 'thorough', 'rhyme', 'rhythm', 'twelfth', 'guarantee', 'vehicle']},
    {'id':'y5-t6-w2-l1','year':'Y5','term':'T6',     'week':2,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 8','lesson':'Lesson 10',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this sequence, children study words from the UKS2 statutory word list.\nThe focus of this lesson is on words containing alternative sounds / graphemes.\nsincere / interfere / privilege / criticise / language\nThese words do not follow clear rules and simply have to be learned.\nsincere – The s sound is represented by the letter c. The ear sound is represented by the alternative ere spelling.\ninterfere – The ear sound is represented by the alternative ere spelling.\nprivilege – The edge sound is represented by the alternative ege spelling.\ncriticise – The s sound is represented by the letter c. The z sound is represented by the letter s.\nlanguage – The ending sounds like ij. This sound is represented by the age grapheme.\nMore regular practice of these words in children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['sincere', 'interfere', 'privilege', 'criticise', 'language', 'rhyme', 'rhythm', 'twelfth', 'guarantee', 'vehicle']},
    {'id':'y5-t6-w2-l2','year':'Y5','term':'T6',     'week':2,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 8','lesson':'Lesson 11',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this sequence, children study words from the UKS2 statutory word list.\nThe focus of this lesson is on words containing alternative sounds / graphemes.\nachieve / ancient / bruise / persuade / restaurant\nThese words do not follow clear rules and simply have to be learned.\nachieve – The long e sound is represented by the ie grapheme.\nThis links to prior learning about ei after c. Here, the long e comes after a different consonant (h) so we apply ie.\nancient – The c / ci produces the sh sound.\nbruise – The oo sound is represented by the ui digraph. The z sound is represented by the letter s.\npersuade – The w sound is represented by the letter u. The word ends with a split digraph (a-e).\nrestaurant – The er sound is represented by the vowels au. Sometimes, this sound is omitted entirely when spoken.\nMore regular practice of these words in children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['achieve', 'ancient', 'bruise', 'persuade', 'restaurant', 'rhyme', 'rhythm', 'twelfth', 'guarantee', 'vehicle']},
    {'id':'y5-t6-w2-l3','year':'Y5','term':'T6',     'week':2,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 8','lesson':'Lesson 12',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this sequence, children study words from the UKS2 statutory word list.\nThe focus of this lesson is on words containing alternative sounds / graphemes.\nforeign / yacht / soldier / queue / amateur\nThese words do not follow clear rules and simply have to be learned.\nHowever, identifying unusual spelling patterns can be helpful.\nFor example, the word amateur ends in the 'ure' sound spelled eur.\nTeachers should also recognise common misconceptions and address these.\nFor example, children may write solider instead of soldier.\nMore regular practice of these words in children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['foreign', 'yacht', 'soldier', 'queue', 'amateur', 'rhyme', 'rhythm', 'twelfth', 'guarantee', 'vehicle']},
    {'id':'y5-t6-w3-l1','year':'Y5','term':'T6',     'week':3,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Mastery 4','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'the ough grapheme',     'explanation':"The 'ough' letter string is a quadgraph, meaning it is a grapheme constructed from four letters.\nThere are not many words that contain this letter string and it represents a range of different sounds; therefore, these words simply need to be learned.\nThe 'ough' letter string generally makes the following sounds:\n- uff (as in enough)\n- off (as in cough)\n- aw (as in bought)\n- oa (as in although)\n- ow (as in bough)\n- oo (as in through)\n- uh (as in thorough)",     'hlWords':['cough', 'enough', 'although', 'through', 'thorough', 'bough', 'fought', 'tough', 'thoroughly', 'thoughtful', 'borough', 'throughout', 'drought', 'plough', 'rough']},
    {'id':'y5-t6-w3-l2','year':'Y5','term':'T6',     'week':3,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Mastery 4','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'the ough grapheme',     'explanation':"The 'ough' letter string is a quadgraph, meaning it is a grapheme constructed from four letters.\nThere are not many words that contain this letter string and it represents a range of different sounds; therefore, these words simply need to be learned.\nThe 'ough' letter string generally makes the following sounds:\n- uff (as in enough)\n- off (as in cough)\n- aw (as in bought)\n- oa (as in although)\n- ow (as in bough)\n- oo (as in through)\n- uh (as in thorough)",     'hlWords':['cough', 'enough', 'although', 'through', 'thorough', 'bough', 'fought', 'thought', 'drought', 'borough', 'trough', 'brought', 'throughout']},
    {'id':'y5-t6-w3-l3','year':'Y5','term':'T6',     'week':3,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Mastery 4','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'the ough grapheme',     'explanation':"The 'ough' letter string is a quadgraph, meaning it is a grapheme constructed from four letters.\nThere are not many words that contain this letter string and it represents a range of different sounds; therefore, these words simply need to be learned.\nThe 'ough' letter string generally makes the following sounds:\n- uff (as in enough)\n- off (as in cough)\n- aw (as in bought)\n- oa (as in although)\n- ow (as in bough)\n- oo (as in through)\n- uh (as in thorough)",     'hlWords':['tough', 'rough', 'enough', 'cough', 'trough', 'dough', 'though', 'although', 'through', 'throughout', 'borough', 'thorough', 'plough', 'bough', 'drought', 'ought', 'bought', 'sought', 'fought', 'nought', 'thought', 'brought', 'wrought', 'breakthrough', 'thoughtfully', 'toughest', 'roughly', 'afterthought', 'thoughtfulness', 'thoroughly', 'thoughtful']},
    {'id':'y5-t6-w4-l1','year':'Y5','term':'T6',     'week':4,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Mastery 5','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'silent letters',     'explanation':'There are many potential silent letters that children may come across.\nHowever, this sequence explores some of the most common ones – and the most common positions in which they occur.\nThe most common rules are as follows:\n1. The silent letter b often follows the letter m at the end of a word (e.g. thumb).\n2. The silent letter k often precedes the letter n at the start of a word (e.g. knight).\n3. The silent letter h often follows the letter w at the start of a word (e.g. white).\n4. The silent letter w often precedes the letter r at the start of a word (e.g. wrong).\n5. The silent letter n often follows the letter m at the end of a word (e.g. autumn).\n6. The silent letter g often precedes the letter n at the start of a word (e.g. gnome) or at the end of a word (e.g. design).\n7. The silent letter t often comes between s and le (e.g. castle) or precedes en at the end of a word (e.g. listen).',     'hlWords':['know', 'knife', 'knitting', 'gnome', 'gnaw', 'gnash', 'wrong', 'written', 'wrinkle', 'which', 'whisper', 'wherever', 'gnarled', 'wreckage', 'knocking', 'whispered', 'whatever', 'writing', 'wriggled', 'knuckles', 'kneepad', 'gnashing', 'whistling', 'knowledge', 'knight', 'white', 'wrecked', 'whipped']},
    {'id':'y5-t6-w4-l2','year':'Y5','term':'T6',     'week':4,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Mastery 5','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'silent letters',     'explanation':'There are many potential silent letters that children may come across.\nHowever, this sequence explores some of the most common ones – and the most common positions in which they occur.\nThe most common rules are as follows:\n1. The silent letter b often follows the letter m at the end of a word (e.g. thumb).\n2. The silent letter k often precedes the letter n at the start of a word (e.g. knight).\n3. The silent letter h often follows the letter w at the start of a word (e.g. white).\n4. The silent letter w often precedes the letter r at the start of a word (e.g. wrong).\n5. The silent letter n often follows the letter m at the end of a word (e.g. autumn).\n6. The silent letter g often precedes the letter n at the start of a word (e.g. gnome) or at the end of a word (e.g. design).\n7. The silent letter t often comes between s and le (e.g. castle) or precedes en at the end of a word (e.g. listen).',     'hlWords':['crumb', 'lamb', 'climb', 'hymn', 'column', 'solemn', 'design', 'align', 'campaign', 'guide', 'guarded', 'guilty', 'tomb', 'assign', 'guessed', 'condemn', 'thumb', 'foreign', 'guest', 'honeycomb', 'designed', 'condemned', 'climbing', 'gifted', 'autumn', 'guitar']},
    {'id':'y5-t6-w4-l3','year':'Y5','term':'T6',     'week':4,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Mastery 5','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'silent letters',     'explanation':'There are many potential silent letters that children may come across.\nHowever, this sequence explores some of the most common ones – and the most common positions in which they occur.\nThe most common rules are as follows:\n1. The silent letter b often follows the letter m at the end of a word (e.g. thumb).\n2. The silent letter k often precedes the letter n at the start of a word (e.g. knight).\n3. The silent letter h often follows the letter w at the start of a word (e.g. white).\n4. The silent letter w often precedes the letter r at the start of a word (e.g. wrong).\n5. The silent letter n often follows the letter m at the end of a word (e.g. autumn).\n6. The silent letter g often precedes the letter n at the start of a word (e.g. gnome) or at the end of a word (e.g. design).\n7. The silent letter t often comes between s and le (e.g. castle) or precedes en at the end of a word (e.g. listen).',     'hlWords':['bristle', 'knotted', 'wreckage', 'glisten', 'designer', 'whistle', 'castle', 'often', 'gnome', 'writing', 'autumn', 'nestle', 'christen', 'knuckle', 'design', 'whenever', 'guilty', 'wrestle', 'listen']},
    {'id':'y5-t6-w5-l1','year':'Y5','term':'T6',     'week':5,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Mastery 6','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'ei after c',     'explanation':"In most words, the rule i before e applies when the two letters appear together.\n(Examples include believe, achieve and shield.)\nHowever, when the long e sound follows the letter c, we apply ei after c.\n(Examples include receipt, deceive and ceiling.)\nThe mnemonic 'i before e except after c' is often used to support children's memory of this rule.\nHowever, explaining that this occurs when the ei is producing the long e sound after a c is helpful to ensure that children can identify words that do not follow this pattern.\nFor example, the word weigh contains ei but not after the letter c. In this word, it makes the long a sound, not the long e sound.\nEqually, the word science contains ie because the two letters (i and e) produce separate sounds. They do not work together to produce the long e sound.",     'hlWords':['retrieval', 'conceited', 'scientist', 'perceive', 'receive', 'achieve', 'deceit', 'ancient', 'interview', 'belief', 'receipt', 'society', 'friendly', 'ceiling', 'conceive', 'believe']},
    {'id':'y5-t6-w5-l2','year':'Y5','term':'T6',     'week':5,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Mastery 6','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'ei after c',     'explanation':"In most words, the rule i before e applies when the two letters appear together.\n(Examples include believe, achieve and shield.)\nHowever, when the long e sound follows the letter c, we apply ei after c.\n(Examples include receipt, deceive and ceiling.)\nThe mnemonic 'i before e except after c' is often used to support children's memory of this rule.\nHowever, explaining that this occurs when the ei is producing the long e sound after a c is helpful to ensure that children can identify words that do not follow this pattern.\nFor example, the word weigh contains ei but not after the letter c. In this word, it makes the long a sound, not the long e sound.\nEqually, the word science contains ie because the two letters (i and e) produce separate sounds. They do not work together to produce the long e sound.",     'hlWords':['achievement', 'conceited', 'deceitful', 'efficient', 'belief', 'receive', 'friendly', 'ceiling', 'scientist', 'fieldwork', 'achieve', 'deceive', 'ancient', 'perceive', 'receipt', 'retrieval', 'society']},
    {'id':'y5-t6-w5-l3','year':'Y5','term':'T6',     'week':5,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Mastery 6','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'ei after c',     'explanation':"In most words, the rule i before e applies when the two letters appear together.\n(Examples include believe, achieve and shield.)\nHowever, when the long e sound follows the letter c, we apply ei after c.\n(Examples include receipt, deceive and ceiling.)\nThe mnemonic 'i before e except after c' is often used to support children's memory of this rule.\nHowever, explaining that this occurs when the ei is producing the long e sound after a c is helpful to ensure that children can identify words that do not follow this pattern.\nFor example, the word weigh contains ei but not after the letter c. In this word, it makes the long a sound, not the long e sound.\nEqually, the word science contains ie because the two letters (i and e) produce separate sounds. They do not work together to produce the long e sound.",     'hlWords':['deceive', 'receipt', 'disbelief', 'ceiling', 'ancient', 'conceive', 'perceived', 'preconceived', 'achievement', 'misperceive', 'deceitfulness', 'inconceivable', 'unbelievable', 'achievable', 'deceivingly']},
    {'id':'y6-t1-w1-l1','year':'Y6','term':'T1',     'week':1,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'words ending in -cious/-tious',     'explanation':"The –cious and –tious suffixes sound like 'shus'. They are usually used to form adjectives from nouns. The adjectives formed usually show that something is 'full of' or 'has the quality of'. If the root word ends in -ce, remove the -ce and replace it with -cious. For example, the word space becomes spacious. In addition, words ending in -cious can have related words ending in -city. For example, the word tenacity is related to the word tenacious. If there is a related word ending in -tion, we often use the -tious suffix. For example, the word nutritious has the related word nutrition. There are, however, some words using these suffixes that simply have to be learned.",     'hlWords':['nutritious', 'ambitious', 'infectious', 'fractious', 'gracious', 'spacious', 'malicious', 'superstitious', 'fictitious', 'cautious', 'repetitious', 'vicious', 'expeditious']},
    {'id':'y6-t1-w1-l2','year':'Y6','term':'T1',     'week':1,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'words ending in -cial/-tial',     'explanation':"The –cial and –tial suffixes sound like 'shul'. They are usually used to form adjectives meaning 'relating to' or 'having the quality of'. The suffix -cial often follows a vowel. Examples include special, crucial and beneficial. The suffix -tial often follows a consonant. Examples include potential, torrential and residential. Two common exceptions to these rules are financial and commercial. Both of the root words (finance and commerce) end in ce, which is removed before the -cial suffix is applied.",     'hlWords':['partial', 'sequential', 'substantial', 'torrential', 'celestial', 'special', 'crucial', 'judicial', 'glacial', 'superficial', 'official', 'influential', 'evidential', 'potential', 'martial', 'beneficial', 'essential', 'differential', 'residential', 'impartial', 'artificial']},
    {'id':'y6-t1-w1-l3','year':'Y6','term':'T1',     'week':1,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':"adding vowel suffixes to words ending in 'fer'",     'explanation':"This revisit sequence looks at two rules for doubling consonants before vowel suffixes. When a word ends in a short vowel sound followed by a single consonant, the consonant is doubled before adding a suffix beginning with a vowel. For example, the word 'map' becomes 'mapped'. This is because the short vowel sound (a) is followed by a single consonant (p). When a word ends in 'fer', we need to work out whether that syllable is stressed – this means whether emphasis is placed on this syllable. When the 'fer' syllable is stressed, the consonant r is doubled (e.g. transferred). When the 'fer' syllable is not stressed, the consonant r is not doubled (e.g. inference).",     'hlWords':['referring', 'preferred', 'transferring', 'referee', 'preference', 'transference', 'preferring', 'reference', 'transferred', 'inferred', 'preferable', 'conference', 'difference']},
    {'id':'y6-t1-w2-l1','year':'Y6','term':'T1',     'week':2,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 4',     'focus':'words containing silent letters',     'explanation':'This revisit sequence looks at words containing silent letters. There are many potential silent letters that children may come across. However, this mastery sequence explores the most common ones – and the most common positions in which they occur. The most common rules are as follows: 1. The letter b often comes after the letter m at the end of a word (e.g. thumb). 2. The letters k or g often come before the letter n at the beginning of a word (e.g. knife / gnome). 3. The letter h often comes after the letter w at the beginning of a word (e.g. which). 4. The letter w often comes before the letter r at the beginning of a word (e.g. write). 5. The letter n often comes after the letter m at the end of a word (e.g. autumn). 6. The letter t often comes between s and le or before en at the end of a word (e.g. castle / listen).',     'hlWords':['lamb', 'thumb', 'bomb', 'crumb', 'climb', 'knife', 'knee', 'knock', 'known', 'gnome', 'gnaw', 'gnarl', 'gnat', 'gnash', 'when', 'where', 'wheel', 'while', 'which', 'write', 'wrote', 'wrist', 'wreck', 'wrong', 'castle', 'whistle', 'hustle', 'thistle', 'wrestle', 'listen', 'often', 'fasten', 'soften', 'glisten', 'autumn', 'solemn', 'hymn', 'column', 'condemn', 'knight', 'knuckle', 'wreckage', 'unfasten', 'gnarled', 'knitting', 'bristle', 'honeycomb', 'climber', 'wrecked']},
    {'id':'y6-t1-w2-l2','year':'Y6','term':'T1',     'week':2,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 5',     'focus':"the 'ough' letter string",     'explanation':"This revisit sequence focuses on the 'ough' letter string. The 'ough' letter string is a quadgraph, meaning it is a grapheme constructed from four letters. There are not many words that contain this letter string and it represents a range of different sounds; therefore, these words simply need to be learned. The 'ough' letter string generally makes the following sounds – uff, aw, off, oa, ow, oo and uh.",     'hlWords':['dough', 'borough', 'through', 'although', 'ought', 'coughed', 'toughen', 'plough', 'enough', 'fought', 'trough', 'drought', 'brought', 'bough', 'thorough', 'thoughtful', 'bought', 'doughnuts', 'tough', 'thoroughly', 'ploughed']},
    {'id':'y6-t1-w2-l3','year':'Y6','term':'T1',     'week':2,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Revisit','lesson':'Lesson 6',     'focus':"words with 'ei' after the letter c",     'explanation':"This revisit sequence looks at words that follow the traditional 'i before e except after c' rule. This rule applies when the word contains the long e (ee) sound. When the long e sound is preceded by a c, the sound is represented by the ei grapheme. For example, in the word receive, the ee sound is represented by the ei grapheme because it occurs directly after the letter c. When the long e sound is not preceded by a c, it can be represented by a variety of other graphemes – the ones in this sequence contain the ie grapheme.",     'hlWords':['deceit', 'receive', 'ceiling', 'conceive', 'receipt', 'achieve', 'series', 'believe', 'hygiene', 'brownie', 'receiver', 'perceived', 'relief', 'priest', 'perceive', 'fields', 'deceitful', 'retrieval', 'conceited', 'receiving', 'unbelievable', 'received', 'thief']},
    {'id':'y6-t1-w3-l1','year':'Y6','term':'T1',     'week':3,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 1','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'words ending in -ant/-ent',     'explanation':"The -ent/-ence/-ency suffixes are more common than the -ant/-ance/-ancy suffixes. These suffixes are used to form nouns (e.g. emigrant) or adjectives (frequent). We often use the -ent suffix after a soft c (innocent), a soft g (emergency) or the 'qu' grapheme (frequent). It is also commonly used for words ending in -er or -ere. The suffix -ent is usually also applied to words ending in -id (accident), -ide (resident) and u (fluent). When a word ends in -ate (e.g. hesitate), we usually apply the -ant suffix (hesitant). Often, when a word ends in -y, the -ant suffix is used. The usual rule of changing y to i applies here (defiant) – with the exception of the word 'occupy' becoming 'occupant'. The suffix -ant usually follows words that end in -or (ignorant), -ct (reluctant) or -lt (consultant). There are, however, many words using these suffixes that simply have to be learned.",     'hlWords':['frequent', 'eloquent', 'infrequent', 'subsequent', 'consequent', 'decent', 'recent', 'percent', 'innocent', 'adjacent', 'urgent', 'pungent', 'diligent', 'emergent', 'negligent', 'hesitant', 'vibrant', 'dominant', 'emigrant', 'resonant', 'observant', 'occupant', 'variant', 'migrant', 'expectant', 'maleficent', 'intelligent', 'complacent', 'immigrant', 'operant', 'newsagent', 'relinquent']},
