    {'id':'y6-t2-w1-l1','year':'Y6','term':'T2',     'week':1,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 2','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'words ending in -ible/-able',     'explanation':'The -able/-ably suffixes are more common than the -ible/-ibly suffixes. The -able/-ably suffixes are often applied if there is a related word ending in -ate or -ation. For example, the word applicable has the related word application. In addition, the -able/-ably suffixes are often applied if a full root word can be heard. For example, the word accept becomes acceptable. When the root word ends in e, the e is removed before applying the -able/-ably suffixes. For example, the word note becomes notable. However, if the root word ends with -ce or -ge, the e must be kept (e.g. changeable). This is because the removal of the e would change the c/g from a soft sound to a hard sound. The -ible/-ibly suffixes are often applied when a full root word cannot be heard (e.g. invisible). There are, however, some exceptions to this rule: reversible, responsible, collectible, sensible, destructible',     'hlWords':['affordable', 'affordably', 'considerable', 'considerably', 'avoidable', 'avoidably', 'acceptable', 'acceptably', 'laughable', 'laughably', 'expectable', 'expectably', 'comfortable', 'comfortably', 'printable', 'portable', 'reasonably']},
