    {'id':'y6-t2-w4-l1','year':'Y6','term':'T2',     'week':4,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 3','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'hyphenated words',     'explanation':'A hyphen is a short, horizontal mark that is joined to join words or parts of words. (It is different to a dash, which is a longer horizontal mark that joins parts of sentences.) Hyphens are used in several different circumstances: - When the final letter of a prefix and the first letter of a root word are the same (re-enter) to avoid double letters - To avoid ambiguity when the hyphen changes the meaning of a word (e.g. resign, meaning to quit and re-sign, meaning to sign again) - To join compound words (e.g. well-known or three-year-old)',     'hlWords':['re-establish', 'rewrite', 'pre-exist', 're-enter', 'co-own', 'unclear', 'dishonest', 're-examine', 'co-owner', 'pre-existing', 're-evaluate']},
