    {'id':'y6-t3-w1-l1','year':'Y6','term':'T3',     'week':1,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 4','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':"Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. your / you're The word your is possessive. It indicates that something belongs to you. Please hand in your book. The word you're is a contraction of you are. I know that you're trying hard.",     'hlWords':['your', "you're", 'their', "they're", 'there', 'whose', "who's", 'stationery', 'stationary', 'morning']},
    {'id':'y6-t3-w1-l2','year':'Y6','term':'T3',     'week':1,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 4','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':"Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. there / their / they're The word there refers to a place or can be used to point out something. Put your shoes over there. The word their shows possession. It indicates that someone owns something. We visited their house yesterday. The word they're is a contraction of they are. They told me they're going to be late.",     'hlWords':['their', "they're", 'there', 'your', "you're", 'whose', "who's", 'stationery', 'stationary', 'morning']},
    {'id':'y6-t3-w1-l3','year':'Y6','term':'T3',     'week':1,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 4','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':"Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. whose / who's The word who's is a contraction of the words who is. Do you know who's coming to the party? The word whose can be used as a determiner or a possessive pronoun. It relates to the ownership of something. Do you know whose coat that is?",     'hlWords':['whose', "who's", 'your', "you're", 'their', "they're", 'there', 'stationery', 'stationary', 'morning']},
    {'id':'y6-t3-w2-l1','year':'Y6','term':'T3',     'week':2,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 4','lesson':'Lesson 4',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':'Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. stationery / stationary The word stationery (containing the letter e) is a noun. It refers to writing and office supplies. She used her stationery to write invitations to her party. The word stationary (containing the letter a) is an adjective. It refers to something that is still and unmoving. The car remained stationary at the traffic lights.',     'hlWords':['stationery', 'stationary', 'your', "you're", 'their', "they're", 'there', 'whose', "who's", 'morning']},
    {'id':'y6-t3-w2-l2','year':'Y6','term':'T3',     'week':2,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 4','lesson':'Lesson 5',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':'Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. morning / mourning The word morning (without the letter u) refers to the early part of the day. Every morning, I eat a healthy breakfast. The word mourning (containing the letter u) refers to a feeling of sadness when something is lost. They were mourning the loss of their friend at the funeral.',     'hlWords':['morning', 'mourning', 'your', "you're", 'their', "they're", 'there', 'whose', "who's", 'stationery']},
    {'id':'y6-t3-w2-l3','year':'Y6','term':'T3',     'week':2,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 4','lesson':'Lesson 6',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':'Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. profit / prophet The word profit refers to the money earned by selling something. We made a profit at the bake sale. The word prophet refers to a person who shares messages from God or predicts the future. The prophet shared stories about kindness.',     'hlWords':['profit', 'prophet', 'your', "you're", 'their', "they're", 'there', 'whose', "who's", 'stationery']},
    {'id':'y6-t3-w3-l1','year':'Y6','term':'T3',     'week':3,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 4','lesson':'Lesson 7',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':'Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. In this unit, homophones containing the letter c are nouns and their corresponding homophones containing the letter s are verbs. advice / advise The word advice (containing the letter c) is a noun. It refers to an idea or suggestion. My parents gave me good advice about the problem. The word advise (containing the letter s) is a verb. It means to give someone else an idea or suggestion. My parents tried to advise me about the problem.',     'hlWords':['advice', 'advise', 'your', "you're", 'their', "they're", 'there', 'whose', "who's", 'stationery']},
    {'id':'y6-t3-w3-l2','year':'Y6','term':'T3',     'week':3,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 4','lesson':'Lesson 8',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':'Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. In this unit, homophones containing the letter c are nouns and their corresponding homophones containing the letter s are verbs. practice / practise The word practice (containing the letter c) is a noun. It refers to a session or learning. We have to go to football practice after school. The word practise (containing the letter s) is a verb. It means to do something to improve. I practise my football skills after school.',     'hlWords':['practice', 'practise', 'your', "you're", 'their', "they're", 'there', 'whose', "who's", 'stationery']},
    {'id':'y6-t3-w3-l3','year':'Y6','term':'T3',     'week':3,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 4','lesson':'Lesson 9',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':'Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. In this unit, homophones containing the letter c are nouns and their corresponding homophones containing the letter s are verbs. device / devise The word device (containing the letter c) is a noun. It refers to a tool, gadget or machine. I used the device to play music. The word devise (containing the letter s) is a verb. It means to create or plan something. Together, we tried to devise a new plan.',     'hlWords':['device', 'devise', 'your', "you're", 'their', "they're", 'there', 'whose', "who's", 'stationery']},
    {'id':'y6-t3-w4-l1','year':'Y6','term':'T3',     'week':4,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Mastery 1','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'words ending in -ant/-ent',     'explanation':"The -ent/-ence/-ency suffixes are more common than the -ant/-ance/-ancy suffixes. These suffixes are used to form nouns (e.g. emigrant) or adjectives (frequent). We often use the -ent suffix after a soft c (innocent), a soft g (emergency) or the 'qu' grapheme (frequent). It is also commonly used for words ending in -er or -ere. The suffix -ent is usually also applied to words ending in -id (accident), -ide (resident) and u (fluent). When a word ends in -ate (e.g. hesitate), we usually apply the -ant suffix (hesitant). Often, when a word ends in -y, the -ant suffix is used. The usual rule of changing y to i applies here (defiant) – with the exception of the word 'occupy' becoming 'occupant'. The suffix -ant usually follows words that end in -or (ignorant), -ct (reluctant) or -lt (consultant). There are, however, many words using these suffixes that simply have to be learned.",     'hlWords':['diligent', 'dominance', 'occupancy', 'subsequent', 'observant', 'migrant', 'indulgent', 'hesitance', 'importance', 'innocence', 'diligence', 'vacancy', 'emergency', 'frequency', 'decency', 'tolerance']},
