    {'id':'y6-t4-w1-l1','year':'Y6','term':'T4',     'week':1,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 5','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this sequence, children study the following words from the UKS2 statutory word list: environment, equipment, government, parliament (all ending with the -ment suffix) especially, frequently, immediately, sincerely (all ending with the -ly suffix) conscience, convenience, excellent, existence, hindrance, nuisance, relevant (all ending in forms of the -ent/-ant suffixes) competition, explanation, profession, pronunciation (all ending in forms of the -tion suffix) These words do not follow clear rules and often contain exceptions to rules. The focus of this sequence is to break the words into syllables, identify trickier parts of each spelling and link the words to their meanings. More regular practice of these words in the children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['equipment', 'environment', 'parliament', 'government', 'immediately', 'sincerely', 'frequently', 'especially', 'conscience', 'existence']},
