    {'id':'y6-t4-w2-l1','year':'Y6','term':'T4',     'week':2,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 5','lesson':'Lesson 4',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this sequence, children study the following words from the UKS2 statutory word list: conscious, disastrous, mischievous (all ending with the -ous suffix) determined, equipped (ending with the -ed suffix – following rules for doubling consonants or removing the letter e) dictionary, secretary (ending with -ary) signature, temperature (ending with -ture) available, vegetable (ending with the -able suffix) These words do not follow clear rules and often contain exceptions to rules. The focus of this sequence is to break the words into syllables, identify trickier parts of each spelling and link the words to their meanings and roots. More regular practice of these words in the children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['conscience', 'conscious', 'disaster', 'disastrous', 'mischief', 'mischievous', 'equipment', 'environment', 'parliament', 'government']},
    {'id':'y6-t4-w2-l2','year':'Y6','term':'T4',     'week':2,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 5','lesson':'Lesson 5',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this sequence, children study the following words from the UKS2 statutory word list: conscious, disastrous, mischievous (all ending with the -ous suffix) determined, equipped (ending with the -ed suffix – following rules for doubling consonants or removing the letter e) dictionary, secretary (ending with -ary) signature, temperature (ending with -ture) available, vegetable (ending with the -able suffix) These words do not follow clear rules and often contain exceptions to rules. The focus of this sequence is to break the words into syllables, identify trickier parts of each spelling and link the words to their meanings and roots. More regular practice of these words in the children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['determine', 'determined', 'equip', 'equipped', 'diction', 'dictionary', 'secret', 'secretary', 'conscience', 'equipment']},
    {'id':'y6-t4-w2-l3','year':'Y6','term':'T4',     'week':2,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 5','lesson':'Lesson 6',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this sequence, children study the following words from the UKS2 statutory word list: conscious, disastrous, mischievous (all ending with the -ous suffix) determined, equipped (ending with the -ed suffix – following rules for doubling consonants or removing the letter e) dictionary, secretary (ending with -ary) signature, temperature (ending with -ture) available, vegetable (ending with the -able suffix) These words do not follow clear rules and often contain exceptions to rules. The focus of this sequence is to break the words into syllables, identify trickier parts of each spelling and link the words to their meanings and roots. More regular practice of these words in the children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['available', 'signature', 'vegetable', 'temperature', 'conscience', 'equipment', 'environment', 'parliament', 'government', 'immediately']},
    {'id':'y6-t4-w3-l1','year':'Y6','term':'T4',     'week':3,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Sequence 5','lesson':'Lesson 7',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this lesson, children study the following words from the UKS2 statutory word list: awkward / system / curiosity / prejudice / recognise These words all contain alternative or unexpected sounds. In the word awkward, the final syllable sounds like word but is spelled ward. In the word system, the y makes the short i sound and the e sounds like uh. In the word curiosity, the i makes the long e sound and the final syllable sounds like city but is spelled sity. In the word prejudice, the final syllable sounds like diss but is spelled dice. The i does not make the long i sound despite the appearance of the word containing a split digraph (i-e). In the word recognise, the s makes the z sound. This is common in the English language. The focus of this lesson is to identify unusual or unexpected spelling patterns. More regular practice of these words in the children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['prejudice', 'curiosity', 'awkward', 'recognise', 'system', 'conscience', 'equipment', 'environment', 'parliament', 'government']},
    {'id':'y6-t4-w3-l2','year':'Y6','term':'T4',     'week':3,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Sequence 5','lesson':'Lesson 8',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this lesson, children study the following words from the UKS2 statutory word list: controversy / forty / identity / sacrifice / lightning / individual The focus of this lesson is on segmenting these words. Some have some unusual spelling patterns to note. The word forty is a number yet uses the spelling for not four. The first syllable of the word sacrifice sounds like sack but uses the c as ck is often found at the end of a word. The c makes the s sound. The word ends in the split digraph i-e. The word lightning contains the trigraph igh and is unusual for its singular n placed between light and ing. More regular practice of these words in the children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['forty', 'sacrifice', 'identity', 'controversy', 'lightning', 'individual', 'conscience', 'equipment', 'environment', 'parliament']},
    {'id':'y6-t4-w3-l3','year':'Y6','term':'T4',     'week':3,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Sequence 5','lesson':'Lesson 9',     'focus':'UKS2 word list',     'explanation':"In this lesson, children study the following words from the UKS2 statutory word list: average / definite / desperate / develop / stomach / familiar / variety These words all contain the schwa sound – uh – produced by different vowels. This makes the words more challenging to spell as the vowels are less easily identifiable. They also contain other unusual spelling patterns. For example, the word stomach ends with the k sound represented by ch. It is useful to make links to other similar words. For example, the word familiar is similar to the word family. Both come from the Latin familia (meaning household). More regular practice of these words in the children's writing is crucial and can be achieved through dictation sentences.",     'hlWords':['stomach', 'definite', 'average', 'variety', 'develop', 'familiar', 'desperate', 'conscience', 'equipment', 'environment']},
    {'id':'y6-t4-w4-l1','year':'Y6','term':'T4',     'week':4,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Mastery 3','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'hyphens',     'explanation':'A hyphen is a short, horizontal mark that is joined to join words or parts of words. (It is different to a dash, which is a longer horizontal mark that joins parts of sentences.) Hyphens are used in several different circumstances: - When the final letter of a prefix and the first letter of a root word are the same (re-enter) to avoid double letters - To avoid ambiguity when the hyphen changes the meaning of a word (e.g. resign, meaning to quit and re-sign, meaning to sign again) - To join compound words (e.g. well-known or three-year-old) - For compound numbers between 1 and 99 (e.g. twenty-four)',     'hlWords':['re-elect', 'world-famous', 'high-tech', 're-count', 'twenty-nine', 'cold-blooded', 'eighty-seven', 're-evaluate', 're-educate', 'well-known']},
    {'id':'y6-t4-w4-l2','year':'Y6','term':'T4',     'week':4,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Mastery 3','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'hyphens',     'explanation':'A hyphen is a short, horizontal mark that is joined to join words or parts of words. (It is different to a dash, which is a longer horizontal mark that joins parts of sentences.) Hyphens are used in several different circumstances: - When the final letter of a prefix and the first letter of a root word are the same (re-enter) to avoid double letters - To avoid ambiguity when the hyphen changes the meaning of a word (e.g. resign, meaning to quit and re-sign, meaning to sign again) - To join compound words (e.g. well-known or three-year-old) - For compound numbers between 1 and 99 (e.g. twenty-four)',     'hlWords':['high-speed', 'ninety-seven', 're-educate', 'part-time', 'six-year-old', 'fifty-eight', 'co-own', 'well-known', 're-elect', 'world-famous']},
    {'id':'y6-t4-w4-l3','year':'Y6','term':'T4',     'week':4,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Mastery 3','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'hyphens',     'explanation':'A hyphen is a short, horizontal mark that is joined to join words or parts of words. (It is different to a dash, which is a longer horizontal mark that joins parts of sentences.) Hyphens are used in several different circumstances: - When the final letter of a prefix and the first letter of a root word are the same (re-enter) to avoid double letters - To avoid ambiguity when the hyphen changes the meaning of a word (e.g. resign, meaning to quit and re-sign, meaning to sign again) - To join compound words (e.g. well-known or three-year-old) - For compound numbers between 1 and 99 (e.g. twenty-four)',     'hlWords':['re-elect', 'twenty-nine', 'well-known', 'high-quality', 'five-year-old', 'highly-respected', 'twenty-six', 'high-risk', 're-examine', 'forty-five', 'student-led', 'well-organised', 'man-eating', 'little-used', 'small-business', 're-cover', 'forty-six', 'world-famous', 're-educate']},
    {'id':'y6-t4-w5-l1','year':'Y6','term':'T4',     'week':5,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Mastery 4','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':"Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. The word your is possessive. It indicates that something belongs to you. Please hand in your book. The word you're is a contraction of you are. I know that you're trying hard. The word there refers to a place or can be used to point out something. Put your shoes over there. The word their shows possession. It indicates that someone owns something. We visited their house yesterday. The word they're is a contraction of they are. They told me they're going to be late. The word who's is a contraction of the words who is. Do you know who's coming to the party? The word whose can be used as a determiner or a possessive pronoun. It relates to the ownership of something. Do you know whose coat that is?",     'hlWords':['your', "you're", 'their', 'there', "they're", "who's", 'whose', 'stationery', 'stationary', 'morning']},
    {'id':'y6-t4-w5-l2','year':'Y6','term':'T4',     'week':5,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Mastery 4','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':'Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. stationery / stationary The word stationery (containing the letter e) is a noun. It refers to writing and office supplies. She used her stationery to write invitations to her party. The word stationary (containing the letter a) is an adjective. It refers to something that is still and unmoving. The car remained stationary at the traffic lights. morning / mourning The word morning (without the letter u) refers to the early part of the day. Every morning, I eat a healthy breakfast. The word mourning (containing the letter u) refers to a feeling of sadness when something is lost. They were mourning the loss of their friend at the funeral. profit / prophet The word profit refers to the money earned by selling something. We made a profit at the bake sale. The word prophet refers to a person who shares messages from God or predicts the future. The prophet shared stories about kindness.',     'hlWords':['stationery', 'stationary', 'morning', 'mourning', 'profit', 'prophet', 'your', "you're", 'their', 'there']},
    {'id':'y6-t4-w5-l3','year':'Y6','term':'T4',     'week':5,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Mastery 4','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'homophones',     'explanation':'Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. In this unit, homophones containing the letter c are nouns and their corresponding homophones containing the letter s are verbs. advice / advise The word advice (containing the letter c) is a noun. It refers to an idea or suggestion. My parents gave me good advice about the problem. The word advise (containing the letter s) is a verb. It means to give someone else an idea or suggestion. My parents tried to advise me about the problem. practice / practise The word practice (containing the letter c) is a noun. It refers to a session or learning. We have to go to football practice after school. The word practise (containing the letter s) is a verb. It means to do something to improve. I practise my football skills after school. device / devise The word device (containing the letter c) is a noun. It refers to a tool, gadget or machine. I used the device to play music. The word devise (containing the letter s) is a verb. It means to create or plan something. Together, we tried to devise a new plan.',     'hlWords':['advice', 'advise', 'practice', 'practise', 'device', 'devise', 'advises', 'devices', 'advised', 'your']},
    {'id':'y6-t5-w1-l1','year':'Y6','term':'T5',     'week':1,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Revision','lesson':'Lesson 1',     'focus':'silent letters',     'explanation':'There are many potential silent letters that children may come across. The most common rules are as follows: 1. The silent letter b often follows the letter m at the end of a word (e.g. thumb). 2. The silent letter k often precedes the letter n at the start of a word (e.g. knight). 3. The silent letter h often follows the letter w at the start of a word (e.g. white). 4. The silent letter w often precedes the letter r at the start of a word (e.g. wrong). 5. The silent letter n often follows the letter m at the end of a word (e.g. autumn). 6. The silent letter g often precedes the letter n at the start of a word (e.g. gnome) or at the end of a word (e.g. design). 7. The silent letter u often follows the letter g at the start of a word (e.g. guard). 8. The silent letter t often comes between s and le (e.g. castle) or precedes en at the end of a word (e.g. listen).',     'hlWords':['thumb', 'crumb', 'limb', 'hymn', 'column', 'autumn', 'guide', 'guard', 'guest', 'which', 'white', 'whisper', 'gnat', 'gnaw', 'gnome', 'sign', 'align', 'campaign', 'snow', 'knock', 'knight', 'write', 'wreckage', 'wrong', 'listen', 'soften', 'fasten', 'castle', 'nestle', 'rustle', 'written', 'guitar', 'glisten', 'knuckle', 'thistle', 'whenever', 'design', 'climb', 'gnash', 'wrestle', 'whistle', 'writing', 'whisker', 'guessed', 'whale', 'condemn', 'honeycomb', 'knowledge', 'gnarled', 'while']},
    {'id':'y6-t5-w1-l2','year':'Y6','term':'T5',     'week':1,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Revision','lesson':'Lesson 2',     'focus':'Greek & French sounds',     'explanation':'These alternative spellings have Greek and French origins. There are no clear rules for these words. Instead, they have to be learned. k sound spelled ch: The k sound spelled ch has Greek origins. It is often used in school-related words, particularly those linked to the sciences and music. sh sound spelled ch: The sh sound spelled ch has French origins. s sound spelled sc: The s sound spelled sc has Greek (or Latin) origins. This sound is often found before the letters e, i or y.',     'hlWords':['chef', 'machine', 'crochet', 'scissors', 'ascend', 'muscle', 'chemist', 'monarch', 'chorus', 'anchor', 'brochure', 'scenery', 'chalet', 'descend', 'stomach', 'character', 'chandelier', 'fascinate', 'scientist', 'parachute', 'science', 'descending']},
    {'id':'y6-t5-w1-l3','year':'Y6','term':'T5',     'week':1,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Revision','lesson':'Lesson 3',     'focus':'ough letter string',     'explanation':"The 'ough' letter string is a quadgraph, meaning it is a grapheme constructed from four letters. There are not many words that contain this letter string and it represents a range of different sounds; therefore, these words simply need to be learned. The 'ough' letter string generally makes the following sounds: uff (as in enough), off (as in cough), aw (as in bought), oa (as in although), ow (as in bough), oo (as in through), uh (as in thorough).",     'hlWords':['cough', 'enough', 'although', 'through', 'thorough', 'bough', 'fought', 'thought', 'plough', 'rough', 'borough', 'brought', 'dough', 'doughnut', 'breakthrough', 'thoughtlessness', 'roughening', 'overwrought', 'thoroughly', 'thoughtfully', 'throughout', 'besought']},
    {'id':'y6-t5-w2-l1','year':'Y6','term':'T5',     'week':2,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Revision','lesson':'Lesson 4',     'focus':'prefixes',     'explanation':"Prefixes are units of meaning added to the beginning of a root word in order to change its meaning. These prefixes are usually added with no change to the root word. The un- prefix usually means 'not'. For example, the word unhappy means not happy. It can, in some cases, mean to 'reverse' something. For example, the word unlock means to reverse locking something. The dis- prefix usually means 'the opposite of' or 'not'. For example, the word disobey is the opposite of the word 'obey' and means to not obey. It can, in some cases, mean 'apart' or 'away'. For example, the word disappear is the opposite of appear – but it also shows that something has gone away. The mis- prefix means 'wrongly' or 'incorrectly'. For example, the word misread means to read something incorrectly. The prefixes in- / il- / im- / ir- all have the same meaning – they mean 'not'. The in- prefix is the most common. The il- prefix is applied when the root word begins with the letter 'l'. For example, the word 'legal' becomes 'illegal'. The ir- prefix is applied when the root word begins with the letter 'r'. For example, the word 'rational' becomes 'irrational'. The im- prefix is applied when the root word begins with the letters 'm' or 'p'. For example, the words 'mature' and 'polite' become 'immature' and 'impolite'. The re- prefix usually means again. It can, in some cases, mean back. The sub- prefix means under. The super- prefix means above or beyond. It is used to show that something is better than or superior to something else. The inter- prefix means between. The anti- prefix means against or the opposite. The auto- prefix means self or own. Note that applying some of these prefixes can sometimes lead to a double consonant. This is caused by the ending consonant of the prefix being the same as the beginning letter in the root word (e.g. illegal).",     'hlWords':['unhappy', 'disagree', 'misread', 'immoral', 'impolite', 'illegal', 'irrelevant', 'inactive', 'unfriendly', 'disappear', 'uncertain', 'misbehave', 'misguide', 'disorder', 'incorrect', 'irrational', 'illogical', 'immortal', 'inaccurate', 'improper', 'superhuman', 'recreate', 'antifreeze', 'international', 'automobile', 'submarine', 'invisible', 'intolerant', 'indirect', 'revisit', 'reconsider', 'rearrange', 'imperfect', 'immovable', 'impatient', 'reconnect', 'supermarket', 'discomfort']},
    {'id':'y6-t5-w2-l2','year':'Y6','term':'T5',     'week':2,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Revision','lesson':'Lesson 5',     'focus':'alternative sounds',     'explanation':"The -sure ending sounds like /ʒə/ (which sounds like zhuh). This means that it makes a soft 'z' sound (ʒ) followed by the schwa (ə) that sounds like 'uh'. Examples of words ending in -sure include measure, treasure, leisure and enclosure. The -ture ending sounds like /tʃə/ (which sounds like chuh). This means that it makes the 'ch' sound (tʃ) followed by the schwa (ə) that sounds like 'uh'. Examples of words ending in –ture include picture, adventure, structure and creature. Words ending in -ture can be confused with words that end in (t)ch + er. The 'cher' or 'tcher' ending happens when there is a root word ending in -ch (teach) or -tch (watch). These words can have the -er suffix applied, resulting in teacher or watcher. The resulting word (e.g. teacher) sounds the same as the -ture ending. The -gue ending produces the g sound. Examples of words ending in -gue include league, tongue and colleague. The -que ending produces the k sound. Examples of words ending in -que include unique, antique and mosque. There is not a clear rule to follow for these endings – but the sounds they form are clearly different so oral pronunciation is important.",     'hlWords':['league', 'unique', 'treasure', 'creature', 'measure', 'furniture', 'structure', 'leisure', 'adventure', 'departure', 'sculpture', 'enclosure', 'temperature', 'tongue', 'mosque', 'technique', 'colleague', 'fatigue', 'critique', 'antique', 'intrigue', 'dialogue', 'fracture', 'literature', 'boutique', 'opaque', 'statuesque', 'closure', 'pleasure', 'composure', 'analogue', 'prologue', 'meringue', 'catalogue', 'picture']},
    {'id':'y6-t5-w2-l3','year':'Y6','term':'T5',     'week':2,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Revision','lesson':'Lesson 6',     'focus':'ei after c',     'explanation':"In most words, the rule i before e applies when the two letters appear together. (Examples include believe, achieve and shield.) However, when the long e sound follows the letter c, we apply ei after c. (Examples include receipt, deceive and ceiling.) The mnemonic 'i before e except after c' is often used to support children's memory of this rule. However, explaining that this occurs when the ei is producing the long e sound after a c is helpful to ensure that children can identify words that do not follow this pattern. For example, the word weigh contains ei but not after the letter c. In this word, it makes the long a sound, not the long e sound. Equally, the word science contains ie because the two letters (i and e) produce separate sounds. They do not work together to produce the long e sound.",     'hlWords':['perceive', 'ceiling', 'receipt', 'believe', 'shield', 'achieve', 'deceit', 'deceive', 'relief', 'receive', 'fieldwork', 'conceive', 'inconceivable', 'conceitedly', 'overachievement', 'imperceivably', 'unbelievable', 'underachieved', 'deceitful', 'misconceived']},
    {'id':'y6-t5-w3-l1','year':'Y6','term':'T5',     'week':3,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Revision','lesson':'Lesson 7',     'focus':'cious / tious',     'explanation':"The –cious and –tious suffixes sound like 'shus'. They are usually used to form adjectives from nouns. The adjectives formed usually show that something is 'full of' or 'has the quality of'. If the root word ends in 'ce', remove the 'ce' and replace it with -cious. For example, the word space becomes spacious. In addition, words ending in -cious can have related words ending in 'city'. For example, the word tenacity is related to the word tenacious. If there is a related word ending in 'tion', we often use the -tious suffix. For example, the word nutritious has the related word nutrition. There are, however, some words using these suffixes that simply have to be learned.",     'hlWords':['gracious', 'malicious', 'ferocious', 'audacious', 'cautious', 'ambitious', 'infectious', 'fictitious', 'fractious', 'repetitious', 'voracious', 'expeditious', 'spacious', 'officious', 'superstitious', 'vivacious', 'nutritious']},
    {'id':'y6-t5-w3-l2','year':'Y6','term':'T5',     'week':3,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Revision','lesson':'Lesson 8',     'focus':'cial / tial',     'explanation':'The suffix -cial is often applied after a vowel letter. For example, the cial suffix at the end of the word official follows the vowel letter i. The suffix -tial is often applied after a consonant letter. For example, the tial suffix at the end of the word essential follows the consonant letter n. There are some exceptions to this rule: initial / financial / provincial / commercial / palatial. Although three of these words have root words ending in ce, and use the cial suffix, this is not a rule to follow. This is because other words, such as sequential also have root words ending in ce – sequence. Therefore, these exception words simply have to be learned.',     'hlWords':['special', 'crucial', 'official', 'judicial', 'social', 'potential', 'essential', 'partial', 'influential', 'substantial', 'torrential', 'glacial', 'residential', 'beneficial', 'artificial', 'sequential', 'confidential', 'initial', 'financial', 'multiracial', 'palatial', 'commercial', 'unofficially', 'inconsequential', 'confidentiality']},
    {'id':'y6-t5-w3-l3','year':'Y6','term':'T5',     'week':3,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Revision','lesson':'Lesson 9',     'focus':'ible / able',     'explanation':'The -able/-ably suffixes are more common than the -ible/-ibly suffixes. The -able/-ably suffixes are often applied if there is a related word ending in -ate or -ation. For example, the word applicable has the related word application. In addition, the -able/-ably suffixes are often applied if a full root word can be heard. For example, the word accept becomes acceptable. When the root word ends in e, the e is removed before applying the -able/-ably suffixes. For example, the word note becomes notable. However, if the root word ends with -ce or -ge, the e must be kept (e.g. changeable). This is because the removal of the e would change the c/g from a soft sound to a hard sound. The -ible/-ibly suffixes are often applied when a full root word cannot be heard (e.g. invisible). There are, however, some exceptions to this rule: reversible, responsible, collectible, sensible, destructible.',     'hlWords':['suitable', 'readable', 'adaptable', 'treatable', 'acceptable', 'terrible', 'possible', 'invisible', 'audible', 'divisible', 'arguable', 'valuable', 'measurable', 'changeable', 'traceable', 'noticeable', 'reliable', 'variable', 'verifiable', 'reasonable', 'horrible', 'sustainable', 'achievable', 'deniable', 'removable', 'replaceable', 'manageable', 'justifiable', 'notifiable']},
    {'id':'y6-t5-w4-l1','year':'Y6','term':'T5',     'week':4,'weekLesson':1,     'sequence':'Revision','lesson':'Lesson 10',     'focus':'ant / ent',     'explanation':"The -ent/-ence/-ency suffixes are more common than the -ant/-ance/-ancy suffixes. These suffixes are used to form nouns (e.g. emigrant) or adjectives (frequent). We often use the -ent suffix after a soft c (innocent), a soft g (emergency) or the 'qu' grapheme (frequent). It is also commonly used for words ending in -er or -ere. The suffix -ent is usually also applied to words ending in -id (accident), -ide (resident) and u (fluent). When a word ends in -ate (e.g. hesitate), we usually apply the -ant suffix (hesitant). Often, when a word ends in -y, the -ant suffix is used. The usual rule of changing y to i applies here (defiant) – with the exception of the word 'occupy' becoming 'occupant'. The suffix -ant usually follows words that end in -or (ignorant), -ct (reluctant) or -lt (consultant). There are, however, many words using these suffixes that simply have to be learned.",     'hlWords':['urgent', 'diligent', 'negligent', 'innocent', 'adjacent', 'reticent', 'frequent', 'eloquent', 'subsequent', 'tolerant', 'migrant', 'participant', 'hesitant', 'radiant', 'consultant', 'maleficent', 'applicant', 'indulgent', 'dominant', 'innocence', 'importance', 'negligence', 'consequence', 'tolerance', 'observance', 'hesitancy', 'frequency', 'emergency', 'complacency', 'expectancy', 'contingency']},
    {'id':'y6-t5-w4-l2','year':'Y6','term':'T5',     'week':4,'weekLesson':2,     'sequence':'Revision','lesson':'Lesson 11',     'focus':'ous suffix',     'explanation':"The -ous suffix is added to nouns to form adjectives. It means 'full of' or 'having the quality of'. For most root words, simply add -ous (poisonous). If the root word ends in y, change y to i before adding -ous. For example, the word envy becomes envious. If the root word ends in our, change our to or before adding -ous. For example, the word humour becomes humorous. Often, if the root word ends in e, the e should be removed before adding -ous. For example, the word continue becomes continuous. However, if the root word ends in ge, DO NOT remove the e (e.g. outrageous). This is because the e gives the g its 'soft' sound.",     'hlWords':['jealous', 'tremendous', 'populous', 'poisonous', 'dangerous', 'hazardous', 'famous', 'nervous', 'continuous', 'courageous', 'outrageous', 'advantageous', 'various', 'glorious', 'luxurious', 'humorous', 'vigorous', 'glamorous', 'ominous', 'venomous', 'mysterious', 'numerous', 'victorious', 'adventurous', 'mountainous', 'horrendous', 'rigorous']},
    {'id':'y6-t5-w4-l3','year':'Y6','term':'T5',     'week':4,'weekLesson':3,     'sequence':'Revision','lesson':'Lesson 12',     'focus':'ly suffix',     'explanation':'For most root words, we simply add the -ly suffix (because it begins with a consonant). However, there are some exceptions to this, where other rules apply. If the root word ends in y, change y to i before adding -ly. For example, the word happy becomes happily. If the root word ends in -le, change -le to -ly. For example, the word gentle becomes gently. If the root word ends in -ic, use the -ally suffix (instead of -ly). For example, the word magic becomes magically.',     'hlWords':['recently', 'monthly', 'finally', 'happily', 'merrily', 'luckily', 'simply', 'crumbly', 'wrinkly', 'basically', 'musically', 'ironically', 'gently', 'directly', 'primarily', 'flexibly', 'dramatically', 'ordinarily', 'frequently', 'suitably', 'lyrically', 'completely', 'guiltily', 'certainly', 'necessarily', 'desirably', 'subtly', 'typically', 'mightily', 'probably']},
